\par }\pard \qc\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs36\cf6 Foreword to the 2nd Edition
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab A foreword is normally the place where the author of a book expresses thanks and gratitude. I'm not going to do that here. It's not that everyone involved doesn't deserve congratulations and praise, it's just that I already said all those things 
in the foreword to the AD&D }{\i\fs24 Player's Handbook}{\fs24 . Everything I said there is true for this book, too. On to other things.
\par \tab Let's assume that since you're reading this, your are, or plan to be, a Dungeon Master. By now, you should be familiar with the rules in the }{\i\fs24 Player's Handbook}{\fs24 
. You've probably already noticed things you like or things you would have done differently. If you have, congratulations. You've got the spirit every Dungeon Master needs. As you go through this rule book, I encourage you to continue
 to make these choices.
\par \tab Choice is what the AD&D game is all about. We've tried to offer you what we think are the best choices for your AD&D campaign, but each of us has different likes and dislikes. The game that I enjoy may be quite different from your 
own campaign. But it is not for me to say what is right or wrong for your game. True, I and everyone working on the AD&D game have had to make fundamental decisions, but we've tried to avoid being dogmatic and inflexible. The AD&D game is yours, it's mine
, it's every player's game.
\par \tab So is there an "official" AD&D game? Yes, but only when there needs to be. Although I don't have a crystal ball, it's likely that tournaments and other official events will use all of the core rules in these books. Optional rule
s may or may not be used, but it's fair to say that all players need to know about them even if they don't have the memorized.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 Player's Handbook}{\fs24  and the }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24  give you what you're expected to know, but that doesn't mean the game begins 
and ends there. Your game will go in directions not yet explored and your players will try things others think strange. Sometimes these strange things will work; sometimes they won't. Just accept this, be ready for it, and enjoy it.
\par \tab Take the time to have 
fun with the AD&D rules. Add, create, expand, and extrapolate. Don't just let the game sit there, and don't become a rules lawyer worrying about each piddly little detail. If you can't figure out the answer, MAKE IT UP! And whatever you do, don't fall int
o the trap of believing these rules are complete. They are not. You cannot sit back and let the rule book do everything for you. Take the time and effort to become not just a good DM, but a brilliant one.
\par \tab At conventions, in letters, and over the phone I'm often asked for the instant answer to a fine point of the game rules. More often than not, I come back with a question\emdash what do }{\i\fs24 you}{\fs24 
 feel is right? And the people asking the questions discover that not only can they create an answer, but that their answer is as good as anyone else's. The rules are only guidelines.
\par \tab At the beginning of the first }{\i\fs24 Dungeon Master Guide}{\fs24 , Gary Gygax stressed that each of us, working from a common base, would make the AD&D game grow in a variety of different directions. That is more
 true today than ever. Don't be afraid of experimentation, but do be careful. As a Dungeon Master, you have great power, and "with great power comes great responsibility." Use it wisely.
\par 
