\par }{\fs36\cf12 Combat and Encounters
\par }{\fs24 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\tab Encounters are the heart of the AD&D game
. And since encounters with monsters and NPCs often lead to combat, an understanding of what happens during battles is vital. There are several factors the DM will consider in any combat, most of which arise from the circumstances of the encounter. Is any
one surprised? How far apart are the opponents? How many of them are there? Answers to these questions are found in Chapter 11: Encounters. These are questions common to all encounters, whether combat occurs.
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf12 The Combat Round
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab If an encounter escalates i
nto a combat situation, the time scale of the game automatically goes to rounds (also called melee rounds, or combat rounds). Rounds are used to measure the actions of characters in combat or other intensive actions in which time is important.
\par \tab A round is approximately one minute. Ten combat rounds equal a turn (or, put another way, a turn equals 10 minutes of game time). This is particularly important to remember for spells that last for turns, rather than rounds.
\par \tab But these are just approximations--precis
e time measurements are impossible to make in combat. An action that might be ridiculously easy under normal circumstances could become an undertaking of truly heroic scale when attempted in the middle of a furious, chaotic battle.
\par \tab Imagine the simple act of imbibing a}{\i\fs24  healing potion}{\fs24 . First a character decides to drink the potion before retiring for the night. All he has to do is get it out of his backpack, uncork it, and drink the contents. No problem.
\par \tab Now imagine the same thing in the middle of a fight. 
The potion is safely stowed in the character's backpack. First he takes stock of the situation to see if anyone else can get the potion out for him. However, not surprisingly, everyone is rather busy. So, sword in one hand, he shrugs one strap of the pack
 off his shoulder. Then, just as two orcs leap toward him, the other strap threatens to slip down, entangling his sword arm. Already the loose strap keeps him from fully using his shield.
\par \tab Holding the shield as best as possible in front of him, he scrambles
 backward to avoid the monsters' wild swings. He gets pushed back a few more feet when a companion shoulders past to block and give him a little time. So he kneels, lays down his sword, and slips the backpack all the way off. Hearing a wild cry, he instin
ctively swings up his shield just in time to ward off a glancing blow.
\par \tab Rummaging through the pack, he finally finds the potion, pulls it out, and, huddling behind his shield, works the cork free. Just then there is a flash of flame all around him--a fireba
ll! He grits his teeth against the heat, shock, and pain and tries not to crush or spill the potion vial. Biting back the pain of the flames, he is relieved to see the potion is intact.
\par \tab He quickly gulps it down, reclaims his sword, kicks his backpack out of the way, and runs back up to the front line. In game terms, the character withdrew, was missed by one attacker, made a successful saving throw vs. spell (from the}{\i\fs24 
 fireball}{\fs24  spell), drank a potion, and was ready for combat the next round.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 What You Can Do in One Round
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Whatever the precise length of a combat round, a character can accomplish only one basic action in that round, be it making an attack, casting a spell, drinking a potion, or tending to a fallen comrade. The basic action, however, may involv
e several lesser actions.
\par \tab When making an attack, a character is likely to close with his opponent, circle for an opening, feint here, jab there, block a thrust, leap back, and perhaps finally make a telling blow. A spellcaster might fumble for his compone
nts, dodge an attacker, mentally review the steps of the spell, intone the spell, and then move to safety when it is all done. It already has been shown what drinking a potion might entail. All of these things could happen in a bit less than a minute or m
ore, but the standard is one minute and one action to the round.
\par \tab Some examples of the actions a character can accomplish include the following:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx270\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Make an attack (make attack rolls up to the maximum number allowed the character class at a given level)
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Cast one spell (if the casting time is one round or less)
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Drink a potion
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Light a torch
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Use a magical item
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Move to the limit of his movement rate
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Attempt to open a stuck or secret door
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Bind a character's wounds
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Search a body
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Hammer in a spike
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Recover a dropped weapon
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\tab 
There are also actions that take a negligible amount of time, things the character does without affecting his ability to perform a more important task. Examples of these include the following:
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx270\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Shout warnings, brief instructions, or demands for surrender--but not conversations 
\par \tab    where a reply is expected.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Change weapons by dropping one and drawing another.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\fs24  Drop excess equipment, such as backpacks, lanterns, or torches.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\b\fs24 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s5\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\outlinelevel4\adjustright \fs36\cf12 {The Combat Sequence
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 
\par \tab In r
eal life, combat is one of the closest things to pure anarchy. Each side is attempting to harm the other, essentially causing disorder and chaos. Thus, combats are filled with unknowns--unplanned events, failed attacks, lack of communication, and general 
confusion and uncertainty. However, to play a battle in the game, it is necessary to impose some order on the actions. Within a combat round, there is a set series of steps that must be followed. These are:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx270\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24 1.}{\fs24  The DM decides what actions the monsters or NPCs will take, including casting 
\par \tab      spells, if any.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 2.}{\fs24  The players indicate what their characters will do, including and casting of spells.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 3.}{\fs24  Initiative is determined.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 4.}{\fs24  Attacks are made in order of initiative.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab These steps are followed until the combat ends--either one side is defeated, surrenders, or runs away.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 NPC/Monster Determination:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 In the first step, the DM secretly decides in general terms what each opponent will do, such as attack, flee, or cast a spell. He does not announce his decisio
ns to the players. If a spell is to be cast, the DM picks the spell before the players announce their characters' actions.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Player Determination:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Next, the players give a general indication of what their characters are planning to do. This does not have to
 be perfectly precise and can be changed somewhat if the DM decides that circumstances warrant.
\par \tab If the characters are battling goblins, a p
layer can say, "My fighter will attack" without announcing which goblin he will strike. If the characters are battling a mixed group of goblins and ogres, the player has to state whether his character is attacking goblins or ogres.
\par \tab Spells to be cast must also be announced at this time and cannot be changed once the initiative die is rolled. In any situation where the abilities of a character could make a difference, a clear description must be given.
\par \tab Before moving on, the DM will make sure he has a clear 
idea of not only what the player characters are doing, but also what actions any hirelings and henchmen are taking. Once he has a clear view of everything that's likely to happen, the DM can overrule any announced action that violates the rules (or in the
 case of an NPC, is out of character).
\par \tab He is not required to overrule an impossible action, but he can let a character attempt it anyway, knowing full well the character cannot succeed. It is not the DM's position to advise players on the best strategies, 
most intelligent actions, or optimum maneuvers for their characters.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Initiative:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 In the third step, dice are rolled to determine initiative, according to the rules for initiative.
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Resolution:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 In the last step, PCs, NPCs, and monsters make their attacks, spells occur, and any other actions are resolved according to the order of initiative.
\par \tab The above sequence is not immutable. Indeed, some monsters violate the standard sequence, and some situations demand the application of common sense. In these cases the DM's word is final.
\par \tab Here's an example of the combat sequence in action: 
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Rath is leading a party through the corridors of a dungeon. Right behind him are Rupert and Delsenora. Rounding a bend, they see a group of orcs and trolls about 20 feet away. No one is surprised by the encounter.
\par \tab The DM has notes telling him that the orcs are hesitant. He secretly decides that they will fall back and let the trolls fight. The trolls, able to regenerate, are naturally overconfident and step forward to the front ra
nk (cursing the orcs at the same time). Turning to the players, the DM asks, "What are you going to do?"
\par 
\par Harry (playing Rath, a dwarf who hates orcs: "Orcs?--CHARGE!"
\par Anne (playing Delsenora the Mage): "Uh, what!? Wait. Don't do that . . . I was going to . . . now I can't use a fireball spell."
\par \tab DM: "Rath is charging forward. Quick--what are you doing?"
\par Jon (playing Rupert, the half-elf, to Anne): "Cast a spell! (To DM) Can I fire my bow over him?"
\par DM: "Sure, he's short."
\par Jon: "OK, I'll shoot at orcs."
\par DM: "Anne, tell me what Delsenora's doing or she'll lose the round trying to make up her mind."
\par Anne: "Got it! My acid arrow spell at the lead troll."
\par DM: "Fine. Harry, Rath is in front. Roll for initiative."
\par }{\fs24 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf12 Initiative
\par }{\b\fs24 
\par }{\fs24 \tab The initiative roll determines who 
acts first in any given combat round. Initiative is not set, but changes from round to round--combat being an uncertain thing, at best. A character never knows for certain if he will get to act before another.
\par \tab Initiative is normally determined with a single roll for each side in a conflict. This tells whether all the members of the group get to act before or after those of the other side.
\par \tab There are also two optional methods that can be used to determine initiative. Each of these optional methods breaks th
e group action down into more individual initiatives. However, the general method of determining initiative remains the same in all cases.
\par }{\b\fs24 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Standard Initiative Procedure
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab To determine the initiative order for a round of combat, roll 1d10 for each side in 
the battle. Normally, this means the DM rolls for the monsters or NPCs, while one of the players rolls for the PC party. Low roll wins initiative. If more than two sides are involved in combat, the remaining sides act in ascending order of initiative.

\par \tab If 
both or all sides roll the same number for initiative, everything happens simultaneously--all attack rolls, damage, spells, and other actions are completed before any results are applied. It is possible for a mage to be slain by goblins who collapse from 
his}{\i\fs24  sleep}{\fs24  spell at the end of the round.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Initiative Modifiers
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Situational factors can affect who has initiative. To reflect this, modifiers are added to or subtracted from the initiative die roll.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 Table 40: 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Standard Modifiers to Initiative}{\fs24\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx3240\adjustright {\b\fs24 Situation\tab Modifier}{\fs24 \tab 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx3240\outlinelevel0\adjustright {Hasted\tab    -2\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx3240\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 Slowed\tab   +2\tab 
\par On higher ground\tab    -1\tab 
\par Set to receive a charge\tab    -2\tab 
\par Wading or slippery footing\tab   +2\tab 
\par Wading in deep water\tab   +4\tab 
\par Foreign environment*\tab   +6\tab 
\par Hindered (tangled, climbing)\tab   +3\tab 
\par Waiting (}{\i\fs24 Player's Handbook}{\fs24 )\tab   +1\tab 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab * This applies to situations in which the party is in a different environment (swimming underwater without the aid of a }{\i\fs24 ring of free movement}{\fs24 , for example).
\par 
\par \tab Everyone in the party who will be involved in the round's action must qualify for the modifier. For exam
ple, all members of a party must be on higher ground than the opposition to get the higher ground modifier. The DM should ask each player where his character is standing for clarification.
\par \tab The side with the lowest modified roll on 1d10 has the initiative and acts first.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Continuing the example above, the DM decides that one initiative roll is sufficient for each group and no modifiers are needed. Although Rath is charging, the orcs and trolls are too busy rearranging their lines to be set to receive his c
harge. Therefore, the -2 to receive charge is not used.
\par \tab Harry, rolling for the player characters, gets a 7 on a 10-sided die. The DM rolls a 10. The player characters, having the lowest number, act first.
\par \tab Delsenora's acid arrow strikes one of the trolls just as Rath takes a swing at the last of the fleeing orcs. A bowshot from Rupert drops another one of the creatures as it takes its position in the second rank. Now the monsters strike back.
\par \tab The orcs manage to finish forming their line. Enraged by the acid, the lead troll tears into Rath, hurting him badly. The others swarm around him, attempting to tear him limb from limb.
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\b\fs24 Table 41: 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf6 Optional Modifiers to Initiative
\par }{\fs24 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx5040\adjustright {\b\fs24 Situation}{\fs24 \tab        }{\b\fs24 Modifier}{\fs24 \tab 
\par Attacking with weapon\tab    Weapon speed\tab 
\par Breath weapon\tab            +1\tab 
\par Casting a spell\tab      Casting time\tab 
\par Creature size (monsters with 
\par     natural weapons only)*\tab 
\par      Tiny\tab              0\tab 
\par      Small\tab            +3\tab 
\par      Medium\tab            +3\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx5040\outlinelevel0\adjustright {     Large\tab            +6\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx5040\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24      Huge\tab            +9\tab 
\par      Gargantuan\tab          +12\tab 
\par Innate spell ability\tab            +3\tab 
\par Magical items** \tab \tab 
\par      Miscellaneous magic\tab            +3\tab 
\par      Potion\tab            +4\tab 
\par      Ring\tab            +3\tab 
\par      Rods\tab            +1\tab 
\par      Scroll\tab Casting time of spell\tab 
\par      Stave\tab           +2\tab 
\par      Wand\tab           +3\tab 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab * This applies only to creatures fighting with natural weapons--claws, bites, etc. Creatures using weapons use the speed factor of the weapon, regardless of the creature's size.
\par \tab ** Use the initiative modifier listed unless the item description says otherwise.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf6 Group Initiative (Optional Rule)
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Some people believe that using a single initiative roll for everyone on the same side is too unrealistic. It is, admittedly, a simplification, a way to keep down the number of die rolls required in 
a single round. But it allows for much faster combat.
\par \tab However, the actions of different characters, the types of weapons they use, and the situation can all be factors in determining initiative.
\par \tab Using this optional method, one initiative die roll is made for each side in the fight. However, more modifiers are applied to this roll, according to the actions of individual characters. These modifiers are listed on Table 41.
\par \tab Some of the modifiers depend on ability, spell, and weapon. Characters casting spells
 (but not monsters using innate abilities) must add the spellcasting time to the die roll. Characters attacking with weapons add the weapons' speed factors to the die roll (see the}{\i\fs24  Player's Handbook}{\fs24 
 for information on speed factors). All other modifiers are applied according to each individual's situation.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab In the second round of the combat, the DM decides to use the modified group initiative. Rath is surrounded by trolls, and he is not in the best of health. The rest of the party has yet to close with the monsters.
\par \tab The DM decides that one troll will continue attacking Rath, with the help of the orcs, while the other trolls move to block reinforcements. In particular, the troll burned by the acid arrow is looking for revenge. The DM then turns to the playe
rs for their actions.
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\i\fs24 Players (all at once): "I'm going to . . ." "Is he going? .. ." "I'm casting a . . ."
\par DM (shouting): "One at a time! Rath?"
\par Harry: "I'll blow my horn of blasting."
\par DM: "It'll take time to dig it out."
\par Harry: "I don't care, I'm doing it."
\par Jon: "Draw my sword and attack one of the trolls!"
\par DM: "Anne?"
\par Anne (not paying attention to the other two): "Cast a fireball spell."
\par Harry and Jon: "NO! DON'T!"
\par DM: "Well, is that what you're doing? Quickly!"
\par Anne: "No, I'll cast a haste spell! Centered on me, so Rupert and Rath are just at the edge and are caught in the spell, too."
\par DM: "Okay. Harry, roll initiative and everyone modify for your actions."
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab Harry rolls 1d10 and gets a 6. The DM rolls for the monsters and gets a 5. Each person's initiative is modified as follows:
\par \tab Rath is using a miscellaneous magical item (modifier +3). His modified initiative is 9 (6 + 3 = 9).
\par \tab Rupert is using a bastard sword +1 with two hands (weapons speed 7 instead of 8 because of the +1). His modified initiative is 13 (6 + 7 = 13).
\par \tab Delsenora is casting a haste spell (casting time 3). Her modified initiative is the same as Rath's, 9.
\par \tab The trolls are attacking with their claws and bites--large creatures attacking with natural weapons +6. Their modified initiative is 11 (5 + 6 = 11).
\par \tab The orcs are using long swords (weapon speed 5). Their modified initiative is 10 (5 + 5 = 10).
\par \tab After all modified initiatives are figured, the combat round goes as follows: Delsenora (initiative 9) completes her spell at the same time that Rath (9) brings the house down on the orcs with his horn of blasting.
\par \tab After all modified initiatives are figured, the combat round goes as follows: Delsenora (initiative 9) completes her spell at the same time that Rath (9) brings the house down on the orcs with his horn of blasting.
\par \tab The orcs (initiative 10) would have gone next, but all of them have been crushed under falling rock.
\par \tab The three trolls (initiative 11) are unfazed and attack, one at Rath and the other two springing forward, hitting Delsenora and missing Rupert.
\par \tab Finally, Rupert (initiative 13) strikes back. He moved too slowly to block one troll's path to Delsenora, but manages to cut off the second. Things look very grim for the player characters.
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Individual Initiative (Optional Rule)
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab This method of determining initiative is the same as that given earlier, except that each PC, NPC, and monster involved in the fight rolls and then modifies his own initiative. This gives combat a more realistic fe
el, but at the expense of quick play.
\par \tab To players, it may not seem like too much for each to roll a separate initiative die, but consider the difficulties: Imagine a combat between six player characters (each controlled by a player) and five hirelings and 
henchmen against 16 hobgoblins and five ogres (all of which must be rolled by the DM).
\par \tab In addition, each die roll must be modified, according to each individual's actions. The resulting rolls make every combat round a major calculation.
\par \tab This method is not recommended for large-scale combats. It is best used with small battles in which characters on the same side have vastly different speeds.
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab In the third round of combat, the DM decides to use individual initiatives. Each character is involved in his own fight. Cut off from retreat by fallen rock, the trolls attack. The DM asks the players their intentions.
\par 
\par Harry: "Hit him with my hammer +4!"
\par Rupert: "Chop him up."
\par Anne (now in serious trouble): "Cast a burning hands spell."
\par 
\par \tab Each character or monster now rolls 1d10. The rolls and modified results are:
\par \tab Rath rolls a 2 and is attacking with his hammer (weapon speed 0 instead of 4 due to +4) and is hasted (-2), so his modified initiative is 0.
\par \tab Rath's troll rolls a 1 and is attacking with natural weapons (+6 modifier) for a total of 7 (1 + 6 = 7).
\par \tab Rupert rolls a 2 and has a weapon speed of 7 and is hasted (-2) for a modified initiative of 7 (2 + 7 - 2 = 7).
\par \tab Rupert's troll rolls a 5 and modifies this by +6 for an 11 (5 + 6 = 11).
\par \tab Delsenora is very unlucky and rolls a 9. Since she is casting a spell, she gains no benefit form the haste spell this round, as the haste enchantment only affects movement. She has a casting time of 1 for a total of 10 (9 + 1 = 10).
\par \tab The troll fighting Delsenora is very quick and rolls a 1, modified to 7 (1 = 6 = 7).
\par \tab The order of attacks: Rath (initiative 0) strikes with his hammer. Rupert and the two trolls (attacking Rath and Delsenora, all initiative 7) attack immediately after. Rupert hits. The troll
 attacking Rath misses, but Delsenora is hit. Delsenora's spell (initiative 10) would normally happen next, but instead it fizzles. Her concentration was ruined by the blow from the troll. Next, Rupert's troll attacks and misses. Because of the haste spel
l, Rath and Rupert now attack again (in order of initiative), Rath first, then Rupert.
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Multiple Attacks and Initiative
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Combat may involve creatures or characters able to attack more than once in a single round. This may be due to multiple attack forms (c
laws and bite), skill with a weapon, or character level. No matter what the reason, all multiple attacks are handled by one of two methods.
\par \tab When multiple attacks result from different attack forms--for example claws and a bite or bite and tail, or a ranger with his two-weapon combat ability--the attacks occur at the same time. The creature resolves all of its attacks in initiative order.

\par \tab When the attacks are true multiples--using the same weapon more than once--as in the case of a highly skilled fighter, 
the attacks are staggered. Everyone involved in the combat completes one action before the second (or subsequent) attack roll is made.
\par \tab Take, for example, a fighter who can attack twice per round, and say he's battling creatures that can only make one atta
ck. The fighter wins initiative. He makes his first attack according to the rolled initiative order. Then each creature gets its attack. Finally, the fighter gets his second attack.
\par \tab If fighters on both sides in a battle were able to attack twice in a roun
d, their first attacks would occur according to the initiative roll. Their second attacks would come after all other attacks, and would then alternate according to the initiative roll.
\par 
\par \tab As an option, a warrior fighting creatures with less than one Hit Die (1-1 or lower) can make a number of attacks equal to his level (i.e., a 7th-level fighter can make seven attacks). These attacks are handled in order of initiative.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Spellcasting and Initiative
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Casting times for spells can modify initiative rolls, creat
ing a realistic delay for the spellcaster. When a spell's "casting time" parameter is given as a number without any units (e.g., rounds or turns), then that number is added to the caster's initiative roll to determine his modified initiative. When a spell
 requires a round or more to cast, a normal initiative roll is not made--a spell requiring one round to cast takes effect at the end of the current round, after all other actions are completed.
\par \tab Spells that require more than one round to cast involve some b
ookkeeping. The DM or one of the players must keep track of the rounds spent in casting. If the spellcasting character is disturbed during this time, the spell is lost. If all goes well, the spell takes effect at the very end of the last round of the requ
ired casting time. Thus, a spell requiring 10 minutes to cast would require 10 combat rounds, and wouldn't take effect until the very end of the 10th round.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Weapon Speed and Initiative (Optional Rule)
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab 
Each time a character swings a weapon, he places himself out of position to make his next attack. Swinging a hammer is not as simple as tapping in a nail. A warhammer is heavy. Swing it in one direction and it pulls in that direction. It has to be brought
 under control and repositioned before it can be swung again. 
\par \tab The user must regain his balance and plant his feet firmly. Only after doing all this is he ready for his next attack. Compare how quickly someone can throw a punch to the amount of time requir
ed to swing a chair to get a good idea of what weapon speed factors are about.
\par \tab Weapon speed factors slow the speed of a character's attack. The higher the weapon speed factor, the heavier, clumsier, or more limited the weapon is. For the most part, weapon
 speed factors apply to all creatures using manufactured weapons. The speed factor of a weapon is added to the initiative roll of the character to get his modified initiative roll.
\par \tab Thus, if the DM decides to use weapon speed factors for player characters,
 they also should be used for giants, orcs, centaurs, and the like. Otherwise the DM isn't being fair to the players. However, creatures with natural weapons are not affected by weapon speed. Their attacks are natural extensions of their bodies, giving th
em much faster recovery and reaction times.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Magical Weapon Speeds
\par }{\fs24 \tab Magical weapons are easier to wield in combat than ordinary ones. Maybe the weapon is lighter or better balanced than normal; maybe it just pulls the character into the proper position of i
ts own volition. Whatever the cause, each bonus point conferred by a magical weapon reduces the speed factor of that weapon by 1. (A }{\i\fs24 sword +3}{\fs24 
 reduces the weapon speed factor by 3, for example.) When a weapon has two bonuses, the lesser one is used. No weapon can have a speed factor of less than 0.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf12 Attacking
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Once characters decide to attack and the order of initiative has been determined, it is time to resolve all the action. Many factors must be considered in each attack: How many people can surround a 
character? Will a shield block an attack from the rear? Can a character run across a chamber, dodging attackers, in a single round? Can a character win without killing his foe? Is it possible to block an attack?
\par \tab Although the mechanics of combat are very s
imple, there are many different and unusual situations that come up during role-playing battles. Every battle is unique. One key to DMing memorable combat scenes is to remember that not every situation can be anticipated; you just have to combine the rule
s here with good judgment.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf6 Number of Attackers}{\fs36\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab There is a limit to how many attackers can surround a single target. Many factors come into play, notably the relative size of the opponent, the length of the weapons used, and physical obstructions in the a
rea. The obstructions will vary from battlefield to battlefield.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf6 Facing}{\fs36\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Each character or creature is assumed to have a front, flanks, and rear. When creatures of equal size are battling, up to six can surround a single figure.
\par 
