\par }{\b\fs36\cf6 Appendix 3: 
\par }{\b\fs48\cf6 Magical Item Descriptions
\par }{\fs48 
\par }{\fs24 
\par }{\fs36\cf12 Potions
\par }{\fs24 \tab Potions are typically found in ceramic, crystal, glass, or metal flasks or vials (though you can change this, if you want). Flasks 
or other containers generally contain enough fluid to provide one person with one complete dose to achieve the effects described for each potion below.
\par \tab Opening and drinking a potion has an initiative modifier of 1, but the potion doesn't take effect until
 an additional initiative modifier delay of 1d4+1 has passed. Only then do the full magical properties of the potion become evident. Magical oils are poured over the body and smeared appropriately; this imposes a speed factor delay of 1d4 + 1.
\par \tab Potions can
 be compounded by mages at relatively low cost. However, they must have a sample of the desired potion to obtain the right formula. Furthermore, ingredients tend to be rare or hard to come by. This aspect of potions, as well as the formulation of new ones
 by players, is detailed in the Spell Research rules.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Identifying Potions
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab As a general rule, potion containers should bear no identifying marks, so player characters must sample from each container to 
determine the nature of the liquid inside. However, even a small taste should suffice to identify a potion in some way. Introduce different sorts of potions, both helpful and harmful, to cause difficulties in identification. In addition, the same type of 
potion, when created in different labs, might smell, taste, and look differently.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Combining Potions
\par 
\par }{\fs24 \tab The magical mixtures and compounds that make up potions are not always compatible. The compatibility of potions is tested whenever two potions are actuall
y intermingled, or a potion is consumed by a creature while another such liquid, already consumed, is in effect.
\par \tab Permanent potions have an effective duration of one turn for mixing purposes. If you drink another potion within one turn of drinking one with
 Permanent duration, check on Table 111. The exact effects of combining potions can't be calculated, because of differences in formulae, fabrication methods, and component quality employed by various mages. Therefore, it is suggested that Table 111 be use
d, with the following exceptions:
\par \tab 1. A }{\i\fs24 delusion}{\fs24  potion will mix with anything.
\par \tab 2. A }{\i\fs24 treasure finding}{\fs24  potion will always yield a lethal poison.
\par \tab Secretly roll 1d100 for potion compatibility, giving no clues until necessary. The effects of combining specific potions can be pre-set as a plot device, at your option.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 Table 111: 
\par }{\b\fs36\cf6 Potion Compatibility}{\fs36\cf6 
\par }{\b\fs24 
\par }\pard \ri-187\keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx900\adjustright {\b\fs24 D100\tab 
\par Roll\tab Result}{\fs24 \tab 
\par 01\tab Explosion. If two or more potions are swallowed together, internal damage is 
\par \tab 6d10 hit points. Anyone within a 5-foot radius takes 1d10 points of damage. If the 
\par \tab potions are mixed externally (in a beaker, say), all within a 10-foot radius suffer 
\par \tab 4d6 points of damage, no saving throw.
\par 02-03\tab Lethal poison* results. Imbiber is dead. If externally mixed, a poison gas cloud of 
\par \tab 10-foot diameter results. All within the cloud must roll successful saving throws 
\par \tab vs. poison or die.
\par 04-08\tab Mild poison causes nausea and the loss of 1 point each of Strength and Dexterity, 
\par \tab no saving throw. One potion is cancelled and the other is at half strength and 
\par \tab duration. (Determine randomly which potion is cancelled).
\par 09-15\tab Potions can't be mixed. Both potions are totally destroyed\emdash one cancels the other.
\par 16-25\tab Potions can't be mixed. One potion is cancelled, but the other remains normal 
\par \tab (random selection).
\par 26-35\tab Potions can't be mixed. Both potions function at half normal efficacy.
\par 36-90\tab Potions can be mixed** and work normally, unless their effects are contradictory 
\par \tab (for example, }{\i\fs24 diminution}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 growth}{\fs24 , which will simply cancel each other).
\par 91-99\tab Compatible result. One potion (randomly selected) has 150% its normal efficacy. 
\par \tab The DM can rule that only the duration of the augmented potion is extended.
\par 00\tab Discovery. The mixing of the potions creates a special effect\emdash only one of the 
\par \tab potions will function, but its effects upon the imbiber are permanent. (Note that 
\par \tab some harmful side effects could well result from this, at the DM's discretion.)
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24   
\par \tab * A }{\i\fs24 treasure finding}{\fs24  potion always creates a lethal poison when combined with another potion.
\par ** A }{\i\fs24 delusion}{\fs24  potion can be mixed with all other potions.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Potion Duration
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Unless otherwise stated, the effects of a potion last for four complete turns plus d4 additional turns (4+d4).
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 List of Potions
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Animal Control:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This potion enables the imbiber to empathize with and control the emotions of animals of one type\emdash 
cats, dogs, horses, etc. The number of animals controlled depends upon size: 5d4 animals of the size of giant rats; 3d4 animals of about man-size; or 1d4 animals weighing about \'bd ton or more. The type of anim
al that can be controlled depends upon the particular potion, as indicated by die roll (d20):
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright {\b\fs24 D20 Roll\tab Animal Type}{\fs24 \tab 
\par 1-4\tab mammal/marsupial\tab 
\par 5-8\tab avian\tab 
\par 9-12\tab reptile/amphibian\tab 
\par 13-15\tab fish\tab 
\par 16-17\tab mammal/marsupial/avian\tab 
\par 18-19\tab reptile/amphibian/fish\tab 
\par 20\tab all of the above\tab 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Animals with Intelligence of 5 (low Intelligence) or better are entitled to a saving throw vs. spell. Control is limited to emotions or drives unless some form of communication is possible. Note that many monsters can't be controlled by
 the use of this potion, nor can humans, demihumans, or humanoids (see }{\i\fs24 ring of mammal control}{\fs24 ).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Clairaudience:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This potion empowers the creature drinking it to hear as the 3rd-level wizard spell of the same name. However, the potion can be used to hear e
ven unknown areas within 30 yards. Its effects last for two turns.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Clairvoyance:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This potion empowers the individual to see as the 3rd-level wizard spell, }{\i\fs24 clairvoyance.}{\fs24  It differs from the spell in that unknown areas up to 30 yards distant can be seen. I
ts effects last for one turn.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Climbing:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Imbibing this potion enables the individual to climb as a thief, up or down vertical surfaces. A }{\i\fs24 climbing potion}{\fs24   is effective for one turn plus 5d4 rounds.
\par \tab The base chance of slipping and falling is 1%. Make a percentile check at the halfway point of the climb\emdash 
01 means the character falls. For every 100 pounds carried by the character, add 1% to the chance of slipping. If the climber wears armor, add the following to the falling chance:
\par \tab }{\b\fs24 \tab 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2880\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 Armor\tab Chance to Fall}{\fs24\cf6 \tab 
\par }{\fs24 studded leather\tab   1%\tab 
\par ring mail\tab   2%\tab 
\par scale mail\tab   4%\tab 
\par chain mail\tab   7%\tab 
\par banded or splinted armor\tab   8%\tab 
\par plate mail\tab 10%\tab 
\par field plate\tab 10%\tab 
\par full plate\tab 12%\tab 
\par magical armor, any type\tab   1%\tab 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par    }{\b\fs24\cf6 Delusion:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This potion affects the mind of the character so that he believes the liquid is some other potion (}{\i\fs24 healing,}{\fs24  for example, is a good choice\emdash 
damage is "restored'' by drinking it, and only death or rest after an adventure will reveal that the potion only cause
d the imbiber to believe that he was aided). If several individuals taste this potion, it is 90% probable that they will all agree it is the same potion (or whatever type the DM announces or hints at).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Diminution:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 After drinking this potion, the individual (and everything he's carrying and wearing) diminishes in size\emdash 
to as small as 5% of normal size. The percentage of the potion drunk determines the amount a character shrinks: For example, if 40% of the contents are swallowed, the person shrinks to 60% of
 normal size. The effects of this potion last for six turns plus 1d4+1 turns.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Dragon Control:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This potion enables the individual drinking it to cast what is, in effect, a }{\i\fs24 charm monster}{\fs24  spell upon a particular dragon within 60 yards. The dragon is entitle
d to a saving throw vs. spell, but with a -2 penalty. Control lasts for 5-20 (5d4) rounds. There are various sorts of dragon potions, as shown below:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 D20 Roll\tab Dragon Type}{\fs24\cf6 \tab 
\par }{\fs24 1-2\tab White Dragon control\tab 
\par 3-4\tab Black Dragon control\tab 
\par 5-7\tab Green Dragon control\tab 
\par 8-9\tab Blue Dragon control\tab 
\par 10\tab Red Dragon control\tab 
\par 11-12\tab Brass Dragon control\tab 
\par 13-14\tab Copper Dragon control
\par 15\tab Bronze Dragon control
\par 16\tab Silver Dragon control\tab 
\par 17\tab Gold Dragon control\tab 
