\par }{\fs36\cf6 Use of Scroll Spells
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab When a scroll is copied into a spellbook or read to release its magic, the writing completely and permanently disappears from the scroll. The magic content of the spell is bound up in the writing, and u
se releases and erases it. Thus, reading a spell from a scroll of seven spells makes the item a scroll of six spells. No matter what a player may try, each spell on a scroll is only usable once. Exceptions should be made very rarely and only when you have
 a very special magical item in mind\emdash perhaps a scroll that can be read once per week. This would be potent magic indeed.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Casting Scroll Effects
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab The initiative modifier of a scroll is its reading time. For scroll spells, this is equal to the casting time of the spell. For }{\i\fs24 protection}{\fs24  scrolls, the reading time is given in the explanation of the scroll effects.
\par \tab The only requirements for using a scroll are sufficient light to read by and the actual verbalization of the writings. If the reading of a scroll is 
interrupted, the scroll effect is lost and that spell fades away and is lost. Spell components are unnecessary for the scroll reader, and no adverse effects associated with casting the spell are suffered\emdash 
these requirements or penalties have been fulfilled or suffered by the creator of the scroll.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Protection Scroll Effects
\par 
\par }{\fs24 \tab If a player character has more than one protection scroll, the effects are cumulative, but not the duration. Scrolls that protect against creatures do not create an actual, physi
cal globe. If the user forces the creature into a place from which further retreat is impossible\emdash a corner, for example\emdash 
and then continues forward until the creature would be within the radius of the circle, the creature is not harmed, and the protection is
 considered voluntarily broken and disappears. There is no way in which a protection scroll can be used as an offensive weapon.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Who Can Use Scroll Spells?
\par 
\par }{\fs24 \tab Ability to use scroll spells does not permit a priest to use a wizard spell, or a wizard to use a 
priest spell. Likewise, it does not extend the ability of spell use to non-spell-using characters except with respect to protection scrolls. Anyone can use a protection scroll. Paladins and rangers cannot use priest scrolls.
\par \tab Those characters able to read and employ scroll spells can do so regardless of other restrictions. Once the spell is known, it is not necessary to use a }{\i\fs24 read magic}{\fs24 
 spell in order to invoke its powers. Scrolls can be read even by mages who are unable to employ the spell copied because of an inability to learn it or because it is too high level (although, in the latter case, there is a chance of spell failure).

\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Spell Level Range
\par }{\fs24\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 \tab The level range shown in Table 90 gives the parameters for random determination of spell level for scrolls i
f you choose not to set this yourself. With spell level determined, find the particular spell by consulting the appropriate Spell Tables in the }{\i\fs24 Player's Handbook.}{\fs24 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Cursed Scrolls
\par }{\fs24\cf6 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx180\adjustright {\fs24 \tab Some scrolls bear powerful }{\i\fs24 curses,}{\fs24  placed intentionally or as a result of flawed scroll-making. }{\i\fs24 Cursed}{\fs24  scrolls take effect the instant they are first read. A }{
\i\fs24 cursed}{\fs24  scroll can have any effect the DM desires, although a few should be outright deadly. Suggested }{\i\fs24 curses}{\fs24  include the following:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx180\adjustright {\fs24 \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 Bad luck (-1 on attacks and saving throws).
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character's beard grows 1 inch per minute.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character is teleported away from the rest of the party.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 Random monster appears and attacks.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character is polymorphed into a mouse.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character shrinks to half his normal size.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character is stricken with weakness, halving his Strength score.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character falls into a deep sleep from which he can't be roused.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character develops an uncontrollable appetite.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character must always talk in rhyme (preventing spellcasting).
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character is stricken with cowardice and must make a morale check every time a 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx180\adjustright {\tab     monster is encountered.
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx180\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character's alignment is changed.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character suffers 2-6 points of damage.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character suffers amnesia.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character feels compelled to give away all his belongings.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 The character must save vs. paralyzation or petrification.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab In general, the effects of a curse can be negated or reversed by a }{\i\fs24 remove curse.}{\fs24  Some cases (such as }{\i\fs24 petrification}{\fs24 
) may require the use of other spells. Overcoming a curse should be difficult for the player characters, but not impossible.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Maps
\par 
\par }{\fs24 \tab While not magical, maps are special enough to require careful preparation and use by the DM, hence they are included in the listings of magical items. Maps should rarely be given out randomly\emdash 
the DM must have a suitable map prepared in advance. There is no table to govern the nature of maps. All hint at or imply the existence of great treasures to be found while giving onl
y a vague idea of the risks to be overcome. Some may be genuine, others fake. Because of the wide range of possibilities, the DM should use treasure maps as the springboard for new and exciting adventures.
\par 
