\par \tab 5. Only one of the powers of a }{\i\fs24 ring of elemental command}{\fs24  can be in use at any given time.
\par \tab In addition to the powers described above, the ring gives characters the following abilities:
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Air
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Gust of wind}{\fs24  (once per round)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Fly
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Wall of force}{\fs24  (once per day)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Control winds}{\fs24  (once per week)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Invisibility}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab The ring will appear to be an }{\i\fs24 invisibility ring}{\fs24  until a certain condition is met (having the ring blessed, slaying an air elemental, or whatever the DM determines as necessary to activate its full potential).
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Earth
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Stone tell}{\fs24  (once per day)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Passwall}{\fs24  (twice per day)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Wall of stone}{\fs24  (once per day)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Stone to flesh}{\fs24  (twice per week)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Move earth}{\fs24  (once per week)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Feather fall}{\fs24 
\par \tab 
\par \tab The ring will appear to be a }{\i\fs24 ring of feather falling}{\fs24  until the DM established condition is met.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Fire
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Burning hands}{\fs24  (once per turn)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Pyrotechnics}{\fs24  (twice per day)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Wall of fire}{\fs24  (once per day)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Flame strike}{\fs24  (twice per week)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Fire resistance}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab The ring will appear to be a }{\i\fs24 ring of fire resistance}{\fs24  until the DM-established condition is met.
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Water
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Purify water
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Create water}{\fs24  (once per day)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Water breathing}{\fs24  (5-foot radius)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Wall of ice}{\fs24  (once per day)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Airy water
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Lower water}{\fs24  (twice per week)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Part water}{\fs24  (twice per week)}{\i\fs24 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Water walking}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab The ring will appear to be a }{\i\fs24 ring of water walking}{\fs24  until the DM-established condition is met.
\par 
\par \tab These rings operate at 12th level of experience, or the minimum level needed to perform the equivalent magical spell, if greater. The additional powers have an initiative modifier of +5.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Feather Falling:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring protects its wearer by automatic activation of a }{\i\fs24 feather fall}{\fs24  if the individual falls 5 feet or more. (See the }{\i\fs24 feather fall}{\fs24  spell in the }{
\i\fs24 Player's Handbook.}{\fs24 )
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Fire Resistance:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The wearer of this ring is totally immune to the effects of normal fires\emdash torches, flaming oil, bonfires, etc. Very large and hot fires, molten lava, hell-hound breath, or a }{\i\fs24 
wall of fire}{\fs24  spell will cause 10 hit points of damage per round if the wearer is directly within the conflagration.
\par \tab Exceptionally hot fires such as red-drago
n breath, pyrohydra breath, fireballs, flame strike, fire storm, etc., are saved against with a +4 bonus to the die roll, and all damage dice are calculated at -2 per die, but each die is never less than 1 in any event. As a rule of thumb, consider very h
ot fires as those that have a maximum initial exposure of up to 24 hit points, those of exceptional heat (25 or more hit points).
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Free Action:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring enables the wearer to move and attack freely and normally even when attacked by a }{\i\fs24 web, hold,}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 slow}{\fs24 
 spell, or even while under water. The spells simply have no effect. While under water, the individual moves at normal (surface) speed and does full damage even with cutting weapons (like axes and scimitars) and with smashing weapons (like flails, ha
mmers, and maces), insofar as the weapon used is held rather than hurled. This will not, however, enable breathing under water without further appropriate magic.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Human Influence:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring has the effect of raising the wearer's Charisma to 18 on encounter reactions with humans and humanoids. The wearer can make a }{\i\fs24 suggestion}{\fs24 
 to any human or humanoid (saving throw applies). The wearer can also }{\i\fs24 charm}{\fs24  up to 21 levels/Hit Dice of human/humanoids (saving throws apply) just as if he were using the wizard spell, }{\i\fs24 charm person.}{\fs24 
 The two latter uses of the ring are applicable but once per day. }{\i\fs24 Suggestion}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 charm}{\fs24  has an initiative penalty of +3.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Invisibility:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The wearer of an }{\i\fs24 invisibility ring}{\fs24  is able to become invisible at will, instantly. This nonvisible state is exactly the same as the wizard }{\i\fs24 invisibility}{\fs24 
 spell, except that 10% of these rings have }{\i\fs24 inaudibility}{\fs24  as well, making the wearer absolutely silent. If the wearer wishes to speak, he breaks all silence features in order to do so.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Jumping:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The wearer of this ring is able to leap 30 feet ahead or 10 feet backward or straight up, with an arc of about 2 feet for every 10 feet traveled (see the 1st-level wizard spell, }{\i\fs24 jump}{
\fs24 ). The wearer must use the ring's power carefully, for it can perform only four times per day.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Mammal Control:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring enables its wearer to exercise complete control over mammals with Intelligence of 4 or less (animal or semi-intelligent mammal
s). Up to 30 Hit Dice of mammals can be controlled. The wearer's control over creatures is so great he can even command them to kill themselves, but complete concentration is required. (Note: The ring does not affect bird-mammal combinations, humans, semi
-humans, and monsters such as lammasu, shedu, manticores, etc.). If the DM is in doubt about whether any creature can be controlled by the wearer of this ring, assume it can't be controlled.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Mind Shielding:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring is usually of fine workmanship and wrought from heavy gold. The wearer is completely immune to }{\i\fs24 ESP, detect lie,}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 know alignment.}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Protection:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A }{\i\fs24 ring of protection}{\fs24  improves the wearer's Armor Class value and saving throws versus all forms of attack. A }{\i\fs24 ring +1}{\fs24 
 betters AC by 1 (say, from 10 to 9) and gives a bonus of +1 on saving throw die rolls. The magical properties of a }{\i\fs24 ring of protection}{\fs24  are cumulative with all other magical items of protection except as follows:
\par \tab 1. The ring does not improve Armor Class if magical armor is worn, although it does add to saving throw die rolls.
\par \tab 2. Multiple }{\i\fs24 rings of protection}{\fs24  operating on the same person, or in the same area, do not combine protection. Only one such ring\emdash the strongest\emdash functions, so a pair of }{\i\fs24 protection rings +2}{\fs24 
 provides only +2 protection.
\par \tab To determine the value of a }{\i\fs24 protection ring,}{\fs24  use the following table:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 D100\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \s7\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\outlinelevel6\adjustright \b\cf6 {Roll\tab Level of Protection
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 01-70\tab +1\tab 
\par 71-82\tab +2\tab 
\par    83\tab +2, 5-foot radius protection
\par 84-90\tab +3\tab 
\par    91\tab +3, 5-foot radius protection
\par 92-97\tab +4 on AC, +2 to saving throws
\par 98-00\tab +6 on AC, +1 to saving throws
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab The radius bonus of 5 feet extends to all creatures within its circle, but applies only to their saving throws (i.e., only the ring wearer gains Armor Class additions).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of the Ram:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ornate ring can be of any hard metal, usually a silver alloy or iron. It has the head of a ram (or a buck goat) as its device. Anyone who attempts a }{\i\fs24 detect magic}{\fs24 
 on the ring discovers an }{\i\fs24 evocation}{\fs24  upon it.
\par \tab The wearer can cause the ring to give forth a ram-like for
ce, manifested by a vaguely discernible shape which resembles the head of a ram or goat. This force strikes one target for 1d6 points of damage if one charge is expended, 2d6 points if two charges are used, or 3d6 points if three charges (the maximum) are
 
used. The ring is quite useful for knocking opponents off walls or ladders, or over ledges, among other things. The force of the blow is considerable, and a victim who fails to save versus spell is knocked down. The range of this power is 30 feet. The tar
get of the blow applies adjustments to the saving throw from the following list:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx360\tx4320\adjustright {\fs24 \tab Target smaller than man-sized\tab -1
\par \tab Larger than man-sized\tab +2
\par \tab Strength under 12\tab -1
\par \tab Strength of 18-20\tab +3
\par \tab Strength over 20\tab +6
\par \tab 4 or more legs\tab +4
\par \tab Over 1,000 lbs. weight\tab +2
\par \tab 2 charges expended\tab -1
\par \tab 3 charges expended\tab -2
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx360\tx4320\tx8370\adjustright {\fs24 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab The DM can make circumstantial adjustments according to need. For instance, a fire giant balanced on a narrow ledge should }{\i\fs24 not}{\fs24  gain any benefit from Strength and weight unless he knows that he's about to be s
truck by the force of the ring. This is a case where common sense will serve best.
\par \tab In addition to its attack mode, the }{\i\fs24 ring of the ram}{\fs24  also has the power to open doors as if a person of 18/00 Strength were doing so. If two charges are expended, the effect
 is as for a character of 19 Strength, and if three charges are expended, the effect is as if a 20 Strength were used. Magically }{\i\fs24 held}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 locked}{\fs24  portals can be opened in this manner.
\par \tab Structural damage from the ramlike force is identical to an actual battering ram, with double or triple damage accruing for applications of two or three charges. Magical items struck by the ramlike force must save versus }{\i\fs24 crushing blow}{
\fs24  if three charges are used; otherwise, the force will not affect them. Nonmagical items which are the target of the force save versus }{\i\fs24 crushing blow}{\fs24  from the impact.
\par \tab A ring of this sort will have from 6 to 10 charges when discovered. It can be recharged by a wizard employing }{\i\fs24 enchant an item}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 Bigby's clenched fist}{\fs24  in combination.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Regeneration:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The standard }{\i\fs24 ring of regeneration}{\fs24 
 restores one point of damage per turn (and will eventually replace lost limbs or organs). It will bring its wearer back from death. (If death was caused by poison, however, a saving throw must be successfully rolled
 or the wearer dies again from the poison still in his system.) Only total destruction of all living tissue by fire or acid or similar means will prevent regeneration. Of course, the ring must be worn, and its removal stops the regeneration processes.

\par \tab A rare kind of }{\i\fs24 ring of regeneration}{\fs24  is the }{\i\fs24 vampiric regeneration}{\fs24 
 ring. This bestows one-half (fractions dropped) of the value of hit points of damage the wearer inflicts upon opponents in hand-to-hand (melee, nonmissile, nonspell) combat immediately upon its 
wearer. It does not otherwise cause regeneration or restore life, limb, or organ. For example, if a character wearing the ring inflicts 10 points of damage, he adds five to his current hit point total. The creature struck still loses 10 points.
\par \tab To determine which type of ring is discovered, roll percentile dice:
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 D100 Roll\tab Secondary Power}{\fs24 
\par 01-90\tab }{\i\fs24 ring of regeneration}{\fs24 
\par 91-00\tab }{\i\fs24 vampiric regeneration ring
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\tx8460\adjustright {\fs24 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 \tab In no case can the wearer's hit points exceed his usual maximum.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Shocking Grasp:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ordinary-see
ming ring radiates only a faint, unidentifiable aura of magic when examined, but it contains a strong enchantment, capable of inflicting damage on an opponent. If the wearer touches an enemy with the hand upon which the ring is worn, a successful attack r
oll deliverers 1d8+6 points of damage to the target.
\par \tab After three discharges of this nature, regardless of the time elapsed between them, the ring becomes inert for one turn. When actually functioning, this ring causes a circular, charged extrusion appear on the palm of the wearer's hand.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Shooting Stars:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring has two modes of operation\emdash at night and underground\emdash both of which work only in relative darkness. During night hours, under the open sky, the }{\i\fs24 shooting stars}{
\fs24  ring will perform the following functions:
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Dancing lights}{\fs24  (once per hour).
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Light,}{\fs24  as the spell of the same name (twice per  night), 120-foot range.
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Ball lightning,}{\fs24  as described below (once per night).
\par \tab  }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Shooting stars}{\fs24  (special).
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 ball lightning}{\fs24  function releases 1d4 balls of lightning, at the wearer's option. These glowing globes resemble dancing lights, and the ring wearer controls them as he would control }{\i\fs24 dancing lights.}{\fs24  The
se spheres have a 120-foot range and a four round duration. They can be moved at 120 feet per round. Each sphere is about 3 feet in diameter, and any creature it touches or approaches within 5 feet dissipates its charge (a successful save vs. spell halves
 damage\emdash the contact was across an air gap). The charge values are:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2880\adjustright {\fs24 4 lightning balls\tab 2d4 points damage each
\par 3 lightning balls\tab 2d6 points damage each
\par 2 lightning balls\tab 5d4 points damage each
\par 1 lightning ball\tab 4d12 points damage
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Release can be one at a time or all at once, during the course of one round or as needed throughout the night.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 shooting stars}{\fs24  are glowing missiles with fiery trails, much like a }{\i\fs24 meteor swarm.}{\fs24  Three }{\i\fs24 shooting stars}{\fs24  can be releas
ed from the ring each week, simultaneously or one at a time. They impact for 12 points of damage and burst (as a }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24 ) in a 10-foot diameter sphere for 24 points of damage.
\par \tab Any creature struck takes full damage from impact plus full damage from the sh
ooting star burst. Creatures within the burst radius must roll a saving throw vs. spell to take only one-half damage (i.e., 12 points of damage, otherwise they, too, receive the full 24 points of damage). Range is 70 feet, at the end of which the burst wi
l
l occur, unless an object or creature is struck before that. The shooting stars follow a straight line path. A creature in the path must roll a saving throw vs. spell or be hit by the missile. Saving throws suffer a -3 penalty within 20 feet of the ring w
earer, -1 from 21 feet to 40 feet, normal beyond 40 feet.
\par \tab Indoors at night, or underground, the }{\i\fs24 ring of shooting stars}{\fs24  has the following properties:
\par \tab   }{\i\fs24 Faerie fire}{\fs24  (twice per day)\emdash as the spell
\par \tab   }{\i\fs24 Spark shower}{\fs24  (once per day)
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 spark shower}{\fs24  is a flying cl
oud of sizzling purple sparks, which fan out from the ring for a distance of 20 feet to a breadth of 10 feet. Creatures within this area take 2d8 points of damage each if no metal armor is worn and/or no metal weapon is held. Characters wearing metal armo
r or carrying a metal weapon receive 4d4 points of damage.
\par \tab Range, duration, and are of effect of functions are the minimum for the comparable spell unless otherwise stated. Casting time is 5
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Spell Storing:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A }{\i\fs24 ring of spell storing}{\fs24  contains 1d4+1 s
pells which  the wearer can employ as if he were a spellcaster of the level required to use the stored spells. The class of spells contained within the ring is determined in the same fashion as the spells on scrolls (see "Scrolls''). The level of each spe
ll is determined by rolling 1d6 (for priests) or 1d8 (for wizards). The number rolled is the level of the spell, as follows:
\par \tab Priest: 1d6, if 6 is rolled, roll 1d4 instead.
\par \tab Wizard: 1d8, if 8 is rolled, roll 1d6 instead.
\par \tab Which spell type of any given level is contained by the ring is also randomly determined.
\par \tab The ring empathically imparts to the wearer the names of its spells. Once spell class, level, and type are determined, the properties of the ring are fixed and unchangeable.
 Once a spell is cast from the ring, it can be restored only by a character of appropriate class and level of experience (i.e., a 12th-level wizard is needed to restore a 6th-level magical spell to the ring). Stored spells have a casting time of five.

\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Spell Turning:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
This ring distorts the three normal dimensions, causing many spells cast at the wearer to rebound upon the spellcaster. Sometimes, a spell's entire effect is turned against the caster; sometimes, a portion of the effect rebounds.
\par \tab Some spells are immune from the effects of a }{\i\fs24 ring of spell turning:}{\fs24 
\par \tab 1. Spells that affect an area, and which are not cast directly at the ring wearer, are not turned by the ring.
\par \tab 2. Spells that are delivered by touch are not turned.
\par \tab 3. Magic contained in devices (rods, staves, wands, rings, and other items) that are triggered without spellcasting are not turned. A scroll spell is }{\i\fs24 not}{\fs24  considered a device.
\par \tab When a spell is cast at an individual wearing a }{\i\fs24 ring of spell turning,}{\fs24  1d10 is rolled and the result is multiplied by 10. This score indicates what percentage of the spell has been turned back upon its caster.
\par \tab Once the spell is turned, the effects must be determined. If the spell normally allows a saving throw, the intended target (the one wearing the ring)
 gains an automatic plus equal to the number rolled on the turning die. The caster receives a bonus equal to the number rolled on the turning die. The caster receives a bonus equal to the number rolled on this die subtracted from 10. For example, a }{
\i\fs24 charm person}{\fs24  spell is cast at a character wearing a }{\i\fs24 ring of spell turning.}{\fs24  A 7 is rolled on the die, turning back 70% of the effect. The ring-wearer gains a +7 to his saving throw; the caster has a +3.
\par \tab A saving throw is also allowed for spells which normally do 
not have one if 20% to 80% of the effect is turned. The saving throw adjustment is calculated as given above. No further adjustments are made for race, magical items, or any other condition including existing spells. To save, the character must have a mod
ified die roll of 20 or greater. If the saving throw is made, the effect of the spell is negated. For example, an illusionist casts a }{\i\fs24 maze}{\fs24  spell at a fighter wearing a }{\i\fs24 ring of spell turning.}{\fs24 
 The spell normally allows no saving throw, but the ring turns 70% 
of the effect. The fighter is allowed a saving throw with a +7 modifier. The illusionist must also save, gaining only a +3. The fighter's die roll is 15, which saves (15+7 = 22); the illusionist's die roll is a 16 which, while close, fails (16+3 = 19). Th
e illusionist becomes trapped in his own }{\i\fs24 maze}{\fs24  spell.
\par \tab Once a spell is turned, the effects are divided proportionately between the two targets. If the spell causes damage, determine the damage normally and then assess the amount to each according to the perc
entage determined, rounding fractions to the nearest whole number. If a spell caused 23 points of damage, and 30% of it was turned, the intended victim would suffer 16 points of damage, while the caster would suffer 7. Durations are affected in a similar 
manner. In the above case, the spell duration would be 30% of its normal length for one character and 70% for the other. The effect of permanent spells for both characters remains unchanged.
\par \tab Some spells affect a certain number of levels. When one of these 
is aimed at the ring wearer, the spell must be able to affect as many levels as the wearer and the spellcaster combined. If this condition is fulfilled, then the procedure above applies.
\par \tab If the spellcaster and spell recipient both wear }{\i\fs24 spell turning rings}{\fs24  a resonating field is set up, and one of the following results will take place:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx900\adjustright {\fs24 01-70\tab Spell drains away without effect
\par 71-80\tab Spell affects both at full effect
\par 81-97\tab Both rings permanently lose their magic
\par 98-00\tab Both individuals go through a rift into the Positive Energy plane
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab A ring wearer who wants to receive a spell must remove the }{\i\fs24 ring of spell turning}{\fs24  to be able to do so.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Sustenance:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This magical ring provides its wearer with life-sustaining nourishment even though he or she might go for d
ays without food or drink. The ring also refreshes the body and mind, so that its wearer needs to sleep only two hours per day to gain the benefit of eight hours of sleep.
\par \tab The ring must be worn for a full week in order to function properly. If it is remov
ed, the wearer immediately loses its benefit and must wear it for another week to reattune it to himself. After functioning for any period of seven consecutive days, a }{\i\fs24 ring of sustenance}{\fs24 
 will cease to function for a week while it replenishes itself.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Swimming:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The }{\i\fs24 ring of swimming}{\fs24 
 bestows upon the wearer the ability to swim at a full 21 base speed. (This assumes, of course, that the wearer is clad in garments appropriate for such activity.) The ring further enables the wearer to dive up to 50 feet 
into water without injury, providing the depth of the water is at least 1\'bd
 feet per 10 feet of diving elevation. The wearer can stay underwater for up to four rounds without needing a breath of air. Surface swimming can continue for four hours before a one
 hour (floating) rest is needed. The ring confers the ability to stay afloat under all but typhoon-like conditions.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Telekinesis:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring enables the wearer to manipulate objects in the same manner as the 5th-level wizard spell, }{\i\fs24 telekinesis.}{\fs24  The
 amount of weight the wearer can move varies. Roll percentile dice to find the strength of the ring:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx360\tx1440\tx8280\adjustright {\fs24 \tab 01-25\tab   25 lbs. maximum
\par \tab 26-50\tab   50 lbs. maximum
\par \tab 51-89\tab 100 lbs. maximum
\par \tab 90-99\tab 200 lbs. maximum
\par \tab   00\tab 400 lbs. maximum
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Truth:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 There is little doubt that wearing a }{\i\fs24 ring of truth}{\fs24 
 is a mixed blessing. The wearer can detect any lie told to him, but he is unable to tell any sort of falsehood himself. If the wearer tries to tell a lie, he finds himself speaking the literal truth instea
d. On the plus side, the wearer is able to discern the last lie told by another\emdash in fact, the power of the ring causes the voice of the liar to rise to a falsetto.
\par \tab If the wearer of the ring encounters magic that enables falsehoods to be spoken without detection (such as an }{\i\fs24 undetectable lie}{\fs24  spell or a }{\i\fs24 philter of glibness}{\fs24 
), no lie is detected. However, the ring wearer will find himself unable to hear the voice of the person so influenced, whether or not he is trying to listen. This, of course, reveals the lie indirectly.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Warmth:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
This ring provides its wearer with body heat even in conditions of extreme cold where the wearer has no clothing whatsoever. It also restores damage caused by cold at the rate of one point per turn. It provides a saving throw
 bonus of +2 versus cold-based attacks, and reduces damage sustained by -1 per die.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Water Walking:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring enables the wearer to walk on any liquid without sinking into it\emdash this includes mud, quicksand, oil, running water, and even snow. Up to 1,200 pounds can be supported by a }{
\i\fs24 ring of water walking.}{\fs24  The ring wearer's feet do not actually contact the surface he is walking upon (but oval depressions about 1\'bd inches deep per 100 pounds of weight of the walker will be observed in hardening mud or se
t snow). The wearer moves at his standard movement rate.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Weakness:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This cursed ring causes the wearer to lose 1 point of Strength and 1 point of Constitution per turn until the individual reaches three in each ability. This loss is not noticeable
 until the individual actually observes his weakened state through some exertion (such as combat or heavy lifting). The ring can also make the wearer invisible at will (at the cost of double the standard rate of Strength and Constitution loss). When the a
ffected abilities reach 3, the wearer will be unable to function in his class.
\par \tab Points lost from the ring are restored by rest on a one-for-one basis, with 1 point of each ability lost being restored in one day of rest. The }{\i\fs24 ring of weakness}{\fs24  can be removed only if a }{\i\fs24 remove curse}{\fs24 
 spell, followed by a }{\i\fs24 dispel magic,}{\fs24  is cast upon the ring.
\par \tab There is a 5% chance that this procedure will reverse the ring's effect, changing it to a }{\i\fs24 ring of berserk strength.}{\fs24  This increases Strength and Constitution at a rate of 1 poi
nt per ability per turn, to a maximum of 18 each (roll percentile dice for bonus Strength if the wearer is a warrior). However, once 18 is reached in both abilities, the wearer will immediately melee with any opponent he meets, regardless of circumstances
. Berserk strength is lost when the ring is removed (by casting a }{\i\fs24 remove curse}{\fs24 ), as are Constitution points gained.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Wishes, Multiple:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring contains from 2d4 }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  spells. As with any wish, the DM should be very judicious in handling the reques
t. If players are greedy and grasping, interpret their wording exactly, twist the wording, or simply rule the request is beyond the power of the magic. In any case, the wish is used up, whether or not the wish was granted, and regardless of the DM's inter
pretation of the wisher's request. No wish can cancel the decrees of god-like beings, unless it comes from another such creature.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Wishes, Three:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring contains three }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  spells instead of a variable number. It is otherwise the same as a }{\i\fs24 multiple wish}{\fs24  ring except that 25% (01-25) of }{\i\fs24 
three wish}{\fs24  rings contain only }{\i\fs24 limited wish}{\fs24  spells.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of Wizardry:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring doubles the number of spells a wizard may prepare each day in one or more spell levels. Only wizards can benefit from a }{\i\fs24 ring of wizardry.}{\fs24 
 Other classes, even those with spell ability, can neither use nor understand the working of such a ring. To determine the properties of a given ring use the table below:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx360\tx1440\adjustright {\fs24 \tab 01-50\tab doubles 1st-level spells
\par \tab 51-75\tab doubles 2nd-level spells
\par \tab 76-82\tab doubles 3rd-level spells
\par \tab 83-88\tab doubles 1st-and 2nd-level spells
\par \tab 89-92\tab doubles 4th-level spells
\par \tab 93-95\tab doubles 5th-level spells
\par \tab 96-99\tab doubles 1st- through 3rd-level spells
\par \tab   100\tab doubles 4th- and 5th-level spells
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 \tab 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Ring of X-Ray Vision:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ring gives 
its possessor the ability to see into and through substances that are impenetrable to normal sight. Vision range is 20 feet, with the viewer seeing as if he were looking at something in normal light. X-ray vision can penetrate 20 feet of cloth, wood, or s
imilar animal or vegetable material, and up to 10 feet of stone or some metals (some metals can't be penetrated at all):
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2610\tx4950\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b {\tab Thickness\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2610\tx4950\adjustright \fs20 {\b\fs24 \tab Penetrated per\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2610\tx4950\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b {Substance\tab Round of\tab Maximum\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2610\tx4950\adjustright \fs20 {\b\fs24 Scanned\tab X-Raying\tab Thickness\tab }{\fs24 
\par Animal matter\tab      4'\tab      20'\tab 
\par Vegetable matter\tab      2\'bd'\tab      20'\tab 
\par Stone\tab      1'\tab      10'\tab 
\par Iron, Steel, etc.\tab      1"\tab      10"\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \s1\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2610\tx4950\outlinelevel0\adjustright {Lead, Gold, Platinum\tab     nil\tab      nil\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 
\par \tab It is possible to scan up to 100 square feet of area during one round. Thus, during one round, the wearer of the ring could scan an area of stone 10 feet wide and 10 feet high. Alternatively, he could scan an area 5 feet wide and 20 feet high.
\par \tab Secret compartments, drawers, recesses, and doors are 90% likely to be located by x-ray vision scanning. Even though this ring enables its wearer to
 scan secret doors, traps, hidden items, and the like, it also limits his use of the power, for it drains 1 point of Constitution if used more frequently than once every six turns. If it is used three turns in one hour, the user loses 2 points from his to
tal Constitution score, 3 if used four turns, etc.
\par \tab This Constitution loss is recovered at the rate of 2 points per day of rest. If Constitution reaches 2, the wearer is exhausted and must rest immediately. No activity, not even walking, can be performed until Constitution returns to 3 or better.

\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf12 Rods
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Rods are about three feet long and as thick as your thumb. They are normally found in cases or similar storage places. Rods can be fashioned from metal, wood, ivory, or bone. They can be plain or decorated and carved, tipped, or not.
\par \tab Rods are powered by ch
arges, unless noted otherwise in the description of a particular rod. Each time the rod is used, one or more charge may be expended. Characters do not automatically know the number of charges possessed by an item when it is discovered, although research a
nd spellcasting can reveal this.
\par \tab A rod can sometimes be recharged according to the rules given for constructing magical items if its charges have not totally been used up. When a rod is drained of all charges, it loses all its magical properties and cannot be recharged ever again.

\par \tab When discovered, a rod normally contains 41 to 50 (1d10+40) charges. However, while rods almost never have more charges than this, it is possible to find a rod with significantly fewer charges, particularly if it is captured from
 an enemy who has previously used the item.
\par \tab 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Command Words (Optional Rule)
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab A rod is normally activated when the wielder utters a specific command word. This word acts like a trigger, unleashing the power stored within the item. Since control of a rod de
pends on knowledge of the command word, these are jealously guarded by the owner. An absent-minded wizard can etch the command word on the item or carry it on a piece of paper in his pocket, but this is only rarely done. Most often the character must use 
spells of the divination school or sages to discover the correct command to activate a rod.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 List of Rods
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rod of Absorption:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This rod acts as a magnet, drawing magic spells of any nature (priest or wizard) into itself. It then nullifies their effects and 
stores their potential until the wielder releases this energy in the form of spells of his own casting. The magic absorbed must have been directed at the character possessing the rod. The wielder can instantly detect a spell's level as the rod absorbs the
 spell's energy.
\par \tab A running total of absorbed (and used) spell levels should be kept. For example, a rod that absorbs a 6th-level spell and a 3rd-level spell has a total of nine absorbed levels. The wielder of the rod can use captured spell energy to cast a
ny spell he has memorized, at a casting time of 1, without loss of spell memory. The only restriction is that the levels of spell energy stored in the rod must be equal to or greater than the level of the spell the wielder wants to cast. Continuing the ex
ample above, the rod-wielder could cast a maximum of one 9th-level spell, one 6th-level and one 3rd-level, and so on.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 rod of absorption}{\fs24  can never be recharged. It absorbs 50 spell levels and can thereafter only discharge any remaining potential it migh
t have. The wielder will know that the rod's limit has been reached upon grasping the item. Used charges indicate that it has already absorbed some of its maximum of 50 spell levels and that some of those have been used.
\par \tab Here is a more specific example: A priest uses a }{\i\fs24 rod of absorption}{\fs24  to nullify the effect of a }{\i\fs24 hold person}{\fs24 
 spell cast at him by a mage. The rod has now absorbed three spell levels and can absorb 47 more. The cleric can cast any 1st-, 2nd-, or 3rd-level spell he has memorized, without memory
 loss of that spell, by using the stored potential of the rod. Assume the cleric casts a }{\i\fs24 hold person}{\fs24 
 back. This spell is only 2nd-level to him, so the rod still holds one spell level of potential, can absorb 47 more, and has disposed of two charges permanently.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rod of Alertness:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This magical rod is indistinguishable from a }{\i\fs24 footman's mace +1.}{\fs24 
 It has eight flanges on its macelike head. The rod bestows +1 to the possessor's die roll for being surprised, and in combat the possessor gains -1 on initiative die rolls. If it is grasped firmly, the rod enables the character to }{\i\fs24 
detect alignment, evil, good,}{\fs24  illusions, }{\i\fs24 invisibility, lie,}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 magic.}{\fs24  The use of these detect powers does not expend any of the charges in the rod.
\par \tab If the }{\i\fs24 rod of alertness}{\fs24  is planted in the ground, and the possessor wills it to alertness, the rod will "sense'' any creature intends to harm the possessor. Each of the flanges on the rod's head then cast a }{\i\fs24 light}{\fs24 
 spell along one of the main directions (N, NE, E, etc.) out to a 60-foot range. At the same time, the rod creates the effect of a }{\i\fs24 prayer}{\fs24 
 spell upon all creatures friendly to the possessor in a 20-foot radius. Immediately thereafter, the rod sends forth a mental alert to these friendly creature, warning them of possible danger from the unfriendly creature(s) within the 120-foot radius.

\par \tab Lastly, the rod can be used to simulate the casting of an }{\i\fs24 animate object}{\fs24  spell, utilizing any 16 (or fewer) objects specially designated by the possessor and placed roughly around the perimeter of a 6-foot-ra
dius circle centered on the rod. The selected objects can be 16 shrubs, 16 specially shaped branches, or whatever.
\par \tab All of the rod's protective functions require one charge. The }{\i\fs24 animate object}{\fs24  power require one additional charge, so, if all of the rod's protective devices are utilized at once, two charges are expended.
\par \tab The rod can be recharged by a priest of 16th level or higher, as long as at least one charge remains in the rod when the recharging is attempted.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rod of Beguiling:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This rod enables its pos
sessor to radiate an emotional and mental wave of fellow feeling to all creatures with any Intelligence whatsoever (1 or higher Intelligence). The rod causes all such creatures within a 20-foot radius of the device to regard the wielder as their comrade, 
friend, or mentor (no saving throw).
\par \tab The beguiled creatures will love and respect the rod wielder. They will listen, trust, and obey him, insofar as communication is possible and instructions given do
n't consign the beguilded to needless injury or destruction or go against their nature or alignment. Each charge of the rod beguiles for one turn. It can be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rod of Cancellation:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This dreaded rod is a bane to all classes, for its touch drains i
tems of all magical properties unless a saving throw versus the cancellation is made. Contact is made by making a normal attack roll in melee combat.
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 Saving\tab 
