\par \tab Multi-classed characters use the least favorable category. The rod cannot be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rod of Rulership:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
The individual who possesses this magic rod is able to command the obedience and fealty of creatures within 120 feet when he or she activates the device. From 200 to 500 Hit Dice (or levels of experience) can be ruled, but creatures with 
15 or greater Intelligence and 12 or more Hit Dice/levels are entitled to a saving throw vs. spell. Ruled creatures will obey the wielder of the }{\i\fs24 rod of rulership}{\fs24 
 as if he or she were their absolute sovereign. Still if the wielder gives a command that is ab
solutely contrary to the nature of the creatures commanded, the magic will be broken. The rod has a casting time of 5. Each charge lasts for one turn. The rod cannot be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rod of Security:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Each time a charge is expended, this item creates a non-d
imensional space, a "pocket paradise." There the rod's possessor and as many as 199 other creatures can stay in complete safety for a period of time, the maximum being 200 days divided by the number of creatures affected. Thus, one creature (the rod's pos
sessor) can stay for 200 days; four creatures can stay for 50 days; a group of 60 creatures can stay for three days. All fractions are rounded down, so that a group numbering between 101 and 200 inclusive can stay for one day only.
\par \tab In this "paradise,'' creatures don't age (except from magical causes such as the casting of a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 
 spell), and natural healing and curing take place at twice the normal rate. Fresh water and food (fruits and vegetables only) are in abundance. The climate is comfortable for all creatures involved, so that protection from the elements is not necessary.

\par \tab Activation of the rod causes the wielder and as many creatures as were touched with the item at the time of use to be transported instantaneously to the paradise. (Members of large g
roups can hold hands or otherwise touch each other, allowing all to be "touched'' by the rod at once.)
\par \tab When the rod's effect is cancelled or expires, all of the affected creatures instantly reappear in the location they occupied when the rod was activated
. If something else occupies the space that a traveler would be returning to, then his body is displaced a sufficient distance to provide the space required for "re-entry."
\par \tab The rod can be recharged by the joint efforts of a priest of 16th or higher level and a wizard of 18th or higher level.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rod of Smiting:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This rod is a +3 magical weapon that inflicts 1d8+3 points of damage. Against golems, the rod causes 2d8+6 points of damage, and any score of 20 or better completely destroys the golem. Any hit upon a
 golem drains one charge.
\par \tab The rod causes normal damage (1d8+3) versus creatures of the Outer Planes. Any score of 20 or better draws off one charge and causes triple damage: (1d8+3) x3. The rod cannot be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rod of Splendor:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The possessor of this
 rod is automatically and continually bestowed with a Charisma of 18 for as long as the item is held or carried. Whatever garments the possessor wears (including armor) appear to be of the finest quality and condition, although no special magical benefit 
(such as a change in Armor Class) is enjoyed.
\par \tab If the possessor already has a Charisma score of 18 or greater, the rod does not further enhance this attribute. When the possessor expends one charge, the rod actually creates and garbs him in clothing of the 
finest fabrics, plus adornments of furs and jewels.
\par \tab Apparel created by the magic of the rod remains in existence unless the possessor attempts to sell any part of it, or if any of the garb is forcibly taken from. In either of these cases, all of the appar
el immediately disappears. The garments may be freely given to other characters or creatures, however, and will remain whole and sound afterward. Characters bedecked in a magically created outfit can't replace or add garments by expending another charge
\emdash if the possessor tries this, the charge is simply wasted.
\par \tab The value of any noble garb created by the wand will be from 7,000 to 10,000 gp (1d4+6). The fabric will be worth 1,000 gp, furs 5,000 gp, and jewel trim from 1,000 to 4,000 gp (i.e., 10 gems of 100 
gp value each, 10 gems of 200 gp value each or 20 gems of 100 gp value, and so forth).
\par \tab The second special power of the rod, also requiring one charge to bring about, is the creation of a palatial tent\emdash a huge pavilion of silk encompassing between 1,500 and
 3,000 square feet. Inside the tent are temporary  furnishings and food suitable to the splendor of the pavilion and in sufficient supply to entertain as many as 100 persons.
\par \tab The tent and its trappings will last for one day. At the end of that time, the p
avilion may be maintained by expending another charge. If the extra charge isn't spent, the tent and all objects associated with it (including any items that were taken out of the tent) disappear.
\par \tab This rod cannot be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rod of Terror:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This rod is
 a +2 magical weapon capable of inflicting 1d6 +3 points of damage per hit. Furthermore, the wielder can expend a charge to envelop himself in a terrifying aura. His clothes and appearance are transformed into an illusion of darkest horror, such that all 
w
ithin 30 feet who view him must roll successful saving throws vs. rods or be struck motionless with terror. Those who succeed on their save suffer a -1 penalty to their morales and must make immediate morale checks. However, each time the rod is used, the
re is a 20% chance the wielder will permanently lose 1 point from his Charisma score.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf12 Staves
\par 
\par }{\fs24 \tab Staves are about 5 feet or 6 feet long and as thick as a young sapling\emdash 
about an inch and a half at the base, tapering to an inch at the tip, although they can be of nearly equal diameter throughout. Staves are typically fashioned of wood, often carved, usually metal bound, and likely to be gnarly and twisted. They can be unu
sual or appear to be ordinary.
\par \tab Staves, like wands and rods, are powered by charges. A staff typically has 1d6+19 charges when found. Again, it is possible for a stave to have fewer charges, although almost none have more than this number. Some staves can b
e recharged according to the rules for making magical items. Once all the charges are used, the stave is rendered nonmagical and cannot be recharged.
\par \tab Unless inapplicable or otherwise specified, staves function at the 8th level of magic use. Their spell discharge is that of an 8th-level wizard with respect to range, duration, and area of effect.
\par \tab Damage is nominally 8d6 with respect to fireballs, lightning bolts, etc.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Command Words (Optional Rule)
\par 
\par }{\fs24 \tab If the DM desires, unleashing the power of a stave can re
quire a specific command word or phrase that must be discovered or researched separately from the item itself. This command can be as simple as a single word ("Heal'') or could be as complicated as a rhyming phrase that must be adjusted to fit the circums
tances ("I touch this elf; restore his health," or "In this moment of great toil, I command thee, staff\emdash 
uncoil!"). Since command words are the key to great power, wise owners will memorize them and destroy all other evidence. Indeed, knowledge of command words can be used as a bargaining point in surrender negotiations.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 List of Staves
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff-Mace:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This clerical weapon appears to be a normal wooden staff of the type used when trekking in the wilderness. This item is typically made of bronzewood, reinforced 
by heavy bands and tips of iron. It gives off a very faint aura of alteration magic. Upon command, the }{\i\fs24 staff-mace}{\fs24  takes on one of three forms, as desired by the possessor.
\par 
\par Quarterstaff: }{\i\fs24 quarterstaff +3,}{\fs24  iron-shod
\par Great Mace: }{\i\fs24 footman's mace +1,}{\fs24  iron
\par Mace: }{\i\fs24 horseman's mace +2,}{\fs24  iron
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff-Spear:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
When this seemingly ordinary quarterstaff is examined magically, it will have an aura of alteration. Upon proper command, a long and sharp spear blade will shoot forth from its upper end. This makes the weapon into a
 spear rather than a staff. Upon a second command, the length of the weapon will elongate to a full 12 feet, and the third command will recall it to its original form. The powers and value of each staff-spear are determined randomly when the item is first
 employed:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\tx2880\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b {   To Hit\tab   XP\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\tx2880\adjustright \fs20 {\b\fs24 D20 Roll\tab & Damage\tab Value}{\fs24 \tab 
\par 1-6\tab     +1\tab 1,000\tab 
\par 7-10\tab     +2\tab 1,500\tab 
\par 11-13\tab     +3\tab 2,000\tab 
\par 14-16\tab     +4\tab 2,500\tab 
\par 17-19\tab     +5\tab 3,000\tab 
\par 20\tab     +3*\tab 3,500\tab 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 * Does damage as ranseur (2d4), but still acts as a spear if used to thrust or when set to receive a charge.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of Command:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This device has three functions, only two of which will be effective if the wielder is a wizard; all three work when the staff is in a priest's hands. The three functions are:
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Human influence:}{\fs24  This power duplicates that of the ring of the same name. Each }{\i\fs24 suggestion}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 charge}{\fs24  draws one charge from the staff.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Mammal control/animal control:}{\fs24 This power functions only as }{\i\fs24  mammal control }{\fs24  (as the ring of that name) when the staff is used by a wizard. In the hands of a priest it is a staff of }{\i\fs24  animal control }{
\fs24  (as the potion of that name, all types of animals listed). Either use drains one charge per turn or fraction thereof. 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24  Plant control:}{\fs24  This function duplicates that of the potion of the same name, but for each 10-square-foot ares of plants controlled for one turn or lass, one charge is used. A wizard cannot control plants at all.
\par \tab The staff can be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of Curing:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This device can }{\i\fs24  cure disease, cure blindness, cure wounds }{\fs24  ( 3d6+3 hit points),}{\i\fs24  or cure insanity. }{\fs24 
 Each function drains one charge. The device can bi used once per day on any person (dwarf,elf,gnome,half-elf, halfling included), and no function ma
y be employed more than twice per day(i.e., the staff can function only eight times during a 24-hour period). It can be recharged.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of the Magi:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This potent staff contains many spell powers and other functions. Some of its powers drain charges; others don't. the following powers do not drain charges:
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 detect magic
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 enlarge
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 hold portal
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 light
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\i\fs24  protection form evil/good }{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab The following powers drain one charge per usage:
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 invisibility
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 fireball
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 knock
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 lightning bolt
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\i\fs24  pyrotechnics
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet }{\i\fs24  ice storm
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 web
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 wall of fire 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 dispel magic
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 passwall}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab These powers drain two charges per usage:
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 whirlwind*
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 conjure elemental**
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 plane travel
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 telekinesis***}{\fs24 
\par \tab     
\par \tab   * The whirlwind is identical to that caused by a dijinni.
\par \tab   ** The staff can be used to conjure one elemental of each type per day, each having 8 
\par \tab \tab    Hit Dice.
\par \tab ***Telekinesis is at 8th level also (i.e., 200 pounds maximum weight).
\par 
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 staff of the magi}{\fs24 
 adds a +2 bonus to all saving throw rolls vs. spell. It can be used to absorb wizard spell energy directed at its wielder, but if the staff absorbs energy beyond its charge limit, it will explode as if a "retributive strike" (see below) had been made.
 The spell levels of energy absorbed count only as recharging the staff, but they cannot be redirected immediately, so if absorption is desired, that is the only action possible by the staff wielder that round. Note also that the wielder has no idea how m
any spell levels are cast at him, for the staff does not communicate this knowledge as a }{\i\fs24 rod of absorption}{\fs24  does. Absorbing spells is risky, but absorption is the only way this staff can be recharged.
\par \tab Retributive strike is a breaking of the staff. It must b
e purposeful and declared by the wizard wielding it. When this is done all levels of spell energy in the staff are released in a globe of 30-foot radius. All creatures within 10 feet of the broken staff suffer hit points of damage equal to eight times the
 number of spell levels of energy in the rod (1 to 25), those between 10 feet to 20 feet take 6 x levels, and those 20 feet to 30 feet distant take 4 x levels. Successful saving throws versus magic indicate only one-half damage is sustained.
\par \tab The wizard breaking the staff has a 50% chance of traveling to another plane of existence, but if he does not, the explosive release of spell energy totally destroys him. This staff and the }{\i\fs24 staff of power}{\fs24 
 are the only magical items capable of a retributive strike.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of Power:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The }{\i\fs24 staff of power}{\fs24  is a very potent magical item, with offensive and defensive abilities. The powers below cost one charge each:
\par 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 continual light
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 magic missile or lightning bolt
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 ray of enfeeblement
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 levitation
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 cone of cold or fireball}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab The following powers drain two charges each:
\par 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 shield, 5-foot radius
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 globe of invulnerability
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 paralyzation*}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab * Paralyzation is a ray from the end of the staff extending in a cone 40 feet long and 20 feet wide at the far end.
\par 
\par \tab The DM may assign alternate powers by random die roll.
\par \tab The wielder of a }{\i\fs24 staff of power}{\fs24  gains a +2 bonus to Armor Class and saving throws.
 He may use the staff to smite opponents. It strikes as a +2 magical weapon and inflicts 1d6+2 points of damage; if one charge is expended, the staff causes double damage, but two charges do not cause triple damage.
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 staff of power}{\fs24  can be broken for a retributive strike (see }{\i\fs24 staff of the magi}{\fs24 ). The staff can be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of the Serpent:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 There are two varieties of this staff\emdash the "python'' and the "adder."
\par \tab The python strikes as a +2 magical weapon and inflicts 1d6+2 points of damage when it hits. 
If the priest throws the staff to the ground, it grows from its 6-foot length, becoming a constrictor snake, 25 feet long (AC 3, 49 hit points, movement rate of 9). This happens in one round. The snake will entwine if it scores a hit, the opponent being c
o
nstricted for 2d4+2 points of damage per round. The victim will remain trapped by the python until he dies or the creature is destroyed. Note that the python will return to its owner upon command. If it is destroyed while in snake form, the staff is destr
oyed.
\par \tab The adder strikes as a +1 magical weapon and does 2d2 points of damage when it hits. Upon command the head of the staff becomes that of an actual serpent (AC 5, 20 hit points). This head remains for one full turn. When a hit is scored, damage is not 
increased, but the victim must roll a successful saving throw vs. poison (strength E) or be slain. Only evil priests will employ an adder staff. If the snake head is killed, the staff is destroyed.
\par \tab Neither staff has nor requires charges. Most of these staves\emdash 60%\emdash are pythons.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of Slinging:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This magical quarterstaff appears to be a +1 weapon unless it is grasped by a druid, whereupon its power of slinging becomes evident. This power, which can be employed }{\i\fs24 only}{\fs24 
 by a druid, is activated when one end of 
the staff is touched to a heavy object of roughly spherical shape (a stone, metal ball, pottery crock, etc.) of up to nine inches in diameter and five pounds in weight. The object adheres to the end of the staff, and the wielder need then only swing the s
taff in an overhand arc to release the missile toward a desired target.
\par \tab The missile leaves the staff on the downstroke of the overhand swing and travels in a low, rising trajectory, with the missile going one foot upward for every 10 feet traveled. Of cour
se, the arc may be higher, or the missile aimed so as to travel nearly vertically. (In the latter case, reverse the arcing ratio so that one foot of distance laterally is covered for every 10 feet of vertical rise.) The maximum range of such a missile is 
180 feet, with limits of 60 feet and 120 feet on short and medium range, respectively.
\par \tab This staff also carries charges, and a druid wielding the item can expend one charge and thereby use the staff to hurl a missile of large size, as if the wielder were a 
stone giant (range out to 300 feet, 3d10 points of damage per hit). Whether used as a magical quarterstaff or by employing one of its slinging powers, the staff bestows +1 to the wielder's attack roll and +1 per die to damage dealt out. The weapon may be 
recharged by a druid of 12th or higher level.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of Striking:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This oaken staff is the equivalent of a +3 magical weapon. (If the weapon vs. armor type adjustment is used, the }{\i\fs24 staff of striking}{\fs24 
 is treated as the most favorable weapon type vs. any armor.
) It causes 1d6+3 points of damage when a hit is scored. This expends a charge. If two charges are expended, bonus damage is doubled (1d6+6); if three charges are expended, bonus damage is tripled (1d6+9). No more than three charges can be expended per st
rike. The staff can be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of Swarming Insects:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
A staff of this sort is typically short and thick. When initially obtained or encountered, much of its length is covered with finely done carvings depicting winged biting and stinging insects (b
ees, deerflies, horseflies, wasps, and the like). Any priest character (cleric, druid, shaman, witch doctor, etc.) holding it can command the staff to create a swarm of such insects, at the same time expending one of the staff's charges.
\par \tab Range is 60 yards+10 yards per level of the user. The number of insects produced is 60 plus 10 per level. Every 10 insects will inflict 1 point of damage upon the target victim, regardless of Armor Class, unless the victim is protected by a }{
\i\fs24 force field,}{\fs24  engulfed in flames, etc. Note, however, that the insects will not affect creatures larger than man-sized with a natural Armor Class of 5 or better.
\par \tab When a vulnerable target is attacked by the swarm of flying insects, the target will be unable to do anything other than attem
pt to dislodge and kill the things. The insect attack lasts for one round. Each time the staff is employed, one of the insect-shapes carved into its wooden surface will disappear, so it is easy to determine how many charges are left in the staff. Unlike o
thers of its ilk, a staff of this sort can have as many as 50 initial charges. However, it cannot be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of Thunder & Lightning:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Casual examination of this stout quarterstaff will show it to be exceptional, and if it is magically examined, it
 will radiate an aura of alteration magic. Constructed of wood (ash, oak, bronzewood, or the like) and bound with iron set with silver rivets, it has the properties of a +2 magical weapon without any expenditure of its magical charges. Its other magical p
roperties are as follows:
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Thunder:}{\fs24  The staff strikes as a +3 weapon, and unless the opponent struck saves successfully vs. rods, staves, and wands, he will be }{\i\fs24 stunned}{\fs24  from the noise of the staff's impact\emdash 
unable to take any further action in the round struck, and automatically having last initiative in the following round. This power requires the expenditure of one charge.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Lightning:}{\fs24  A short spark of electricity leaps forth when the opponent is struck, causing no
rmal staff damage, plus 2d6 additional points of damage from shock. Note that the staff might not score a hit, but the electrical discharge discounts any form of metal armor (making the target effectively AC 10 for this purpose), so only such damage might
 apply. This power requires the expenditure of one charge.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Thunderclap:}{\fs24  The staff sends forth a cone of deafening noise, 5 feet wide at the apex, 40 feet long, and 20 feet wide at a point farthest from the source. All creatures within this cone, wholly or 
partially, must roll a successful saving throw vs. rods, staves, and wands or be }{\i\fs24 stunned}{\fs24 
 for 1d2 rounds (unable to attack during this time) and unable to hear for 1d2 additional rounds. Those who save are unable to hear for 1d4 rounds, but suffer no loss of attacks. This function requires the expenditure of two charges.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Lightning Stroke:}{\fs24  A bolt similar to that from a }{\i\fs24 wand of lightning}{\fs24  is generated, but it is of 8d6 strength, causing 16-48 points of damage (rolls of 1 are counted as 2) to those who fail a s
aving throw. The stroke can be single or forked. This function of the rod uses two charges.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Thunder & Lightning:}{\fs24  This power combines the }{\i\fs24 thunderclap,}{\fs24  described above, with a forked lightning bolt as in the }{\i\fs24 lightning stroke.}{\fs24  Damage from the lightning is a 
total of 8d6 with rolls of 1 or 2 counted as rolls of 3, for a range of 24-48 points. A saving throw applies, with deafness and half damage suffered by those who are successful. This power requires the expenditure of four charges.
\par 
\par \tab The casting time required for any function is equal to the number of charges expended; thus, the }{\i\fs24 thunder & lightning}{\fs24  function costs four charges and has an initiative modifier of +4.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of Withering:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The }{\i\fs24 staff of withering}{\fs24  is a +1 magical weapon. A hit from it causes 1d4+1 
points of damage. If two charges are expended when a hit is scored, the creature struck also ages 10 years, its abilities and lifespan adjusted for the resulting age increase. If three charges are expended when a hit is made, one of the opponent creature'
s limbs can be made to shrivel and become useless unless it successfully saves vs. spell (check by random number generation for which limb is struck).
\par \tab Ageless creatures (undead, demons, devils, etc) cannot be aged or withered. Each effect of the staff is c
umulative, so that three charges will score damage, age, and wither. Aging a dwarf is of little effect, while aging a dragon could actually aid the creature.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Staff of the Woodlands:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This sort of staff is always made from oak, ash, or yew, finely grained,
 beautifully carved, and bound with bronze. It is effective only in the hands of a druid. Each such staff has the following powers, with each expending one charge per use:
\par 
\par }{\i\fs24 \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Wall of thorns
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Animal friendship}{\fs24  plus }{\i\fs24 speak with animals
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Animate tree*}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab 
* This function duplicates the ability of a treant to cause a large tree to move at a movement rate of 3 and attack as if it were a largest-sized treant, and in all other respects becoming a virtual treant for eight rounds per charge expended. Note th
at one round is required for the tree to animate, and it will return to rooting on the eighth, so only six of the initial eight rounds are effectively available for the attack function.
\par 
\par \tab In addition to these powers, each }{\i\fs24 staff of the woodlands}{\fs24  has a magic
al weapon value. Those with a lesser value have extra magical powers that do not require charges and can be employed once per day: The +4 staff has no additional powers; the +3 staff also confers the power of }{\i\fs24 pass without trace;}{\fs24 
 the +2 staff confers the powers of }{\i\fs24 pass without trace}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 barkskin;}{\fs24  the +1 staff confers the powers of the +2 staff plus the power of the }{\i\fs24 tree}{\fs24 
 spell. To determine which sort of staff has been discovered, assign even chances for each of the four types.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf12 Wands
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Wands are 1\'bc feet long and slender. They are made of ivory, bone, or wood and are usually tipped with something\emdash metal, crystal, stone, etc. They are fragile and tend to break easily. Because of this, they are often kept in cases.
\par \tab Wands perform at 6th level of experience with respect to the damage they cause, range, duration, area of effect, etc., unless otherwise stated.
\par \tab At the DM's option, 1% of all wands can be trapped to backfire.
\par \tab Wands are powered by charges, each use costing one or more charges (depending on the item). 
When discovered, a wand typically contains 1d20+80 charges. Captured wands taken from a defeated foe often have many fewer charges. Wands never have a greater number of charges than those listed. Most wands can be recharged according to the rules for maki
ng magical items.
\par \tab When a wand runs out of charges, it can no longer be recharged. Furthermore, the DM can rule that the wand immediately crumbles into useless dust (settling the issue) or is now a useless, nonmagical stick.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Command Words (Optional Rule)
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab 
Like rods and staves, wands can require the utterance of a command word (or phrase) to operate, and like these other items, the key is seldom found in the lock. The DM can rule that the command word is etched in magical writing on the wand (requiring a }{
\i\fs24 read magic}{\fs24  to translate) or he can make the characters resort to such methods as }{\i\fs24 commune}{\fs24  spells and expensive sages. If you choose not to use this option, ignore references to command words in the item descriptions below
\emdash all items simply work.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 List of Wands
\par 
\par }{\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Conjuration:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Grasping this device enables a wizard to recognize any cast or written conjuration/summoning spell (}{\i\fs24 unseen servant, monster summoning, c
onjure elemental, death spell, invisible stalker, limited wish, symbol, maze, gate, prismatic sphere, wish}{\fs24 ). The wand also has the following powers, which require expenditure of one charge each:
\par 
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 unseen servant
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 monster summoning*}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab * A maximum of six charges may be expended, one per level of the }{\i\fs24 monster summoning,}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 six monster summoning I,}{\fs24  three }{\i\fs24 monster summoning II,}{\fs24  two }{\i\fs24 monster summoning II,}{\fs24 
 or any combination totaling six. The wizard must be of a sufficient experience level to cast the appropriate }{\i\fs24 summoning}{\fs24  spell.
\par 
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 wand of conjuration}{\fs24  can also conjure up a }{\i\fs24 curtain of blackness}{\fs24 \emdash a veil of total black that absorbs all light. The }{\i\fs24 curtain of blackness}{\fs24 
 can cover a maximum area of 600 square feet (60' x 10', 40' x 15', 30' x 20'), but it must stretch from ceiling to floor, wall to wall. The }{\i\fs24 curtain}{\fs24 
 takes two charges to conjure. The veil of total lightlessness can be penetrated only by physical means or magic.
\par \tab The wand also enables its wielder to construct a }{\i\fs24 prismatic sphere}{\fs24  (or }{\i\fs24 wall}{\fs24 ), one color at a time, red to violet, at a cost of one charge per color.
\par \tab Each function of the wand has an initiative penalty of +5, and only one function per round is possible. The wand may be recharged.
\par 
\par }{\fs24\cf6 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Earth and Stone:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A wand of this sort is typically short and tipped with some form of mineral. It is imbued with the following powers:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx180\tx1800\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\i\fs24 Dig}{\fs24 \tab \'bd charge/use
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Passwall}{\fs24 \tab one charge/use
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Move earth}{\fs24 \tab two charges/use
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab In addition, 50% of all such wands have the following powers:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx180\tx2880\adjustright {\fs24 \tab }{\i\fs24 Transmute mud to rock}{\fs24 \tab one charge/use
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Transmute rock to mud}{\fs24 \tab one charge/use
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Enemy Detection:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
This wand pulses in the wielder's hand and points in the direction of any creature(s) hostile to the bearer of the device. The creature(s) can be invisible, ethereal, astral, out of phase, hid
den, disguised, or in plain sight. Detection range is a 60-foot sphere. The function requires one charge to operate for one turn. The wand can be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Fear:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 When the }{\i\fs24 fear}{\fs24  wand is activated, a pale amber ray springs from the tip of the wand,
 forming a cone 60 feet long by 20 feet in base diameter, which flashes on and instantly disappears. Each creature touched by the ray must roll a successful saving throw vs. wand or react as per the }{\i\fs24 cause fear}{\fs24 
 spell (1st-level priest spell, }{\i\fs24 remove fear}{\fs24  rev
ersal). In other words, creatures affected by the wand turn and move at fastest possible speed away from the wielder for six rounds. Each use costs one charge. It can operate just once per round. The wand can be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Fire:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This wand can function like the following wizard spells:
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Burning hands:}{\fs24  The wand emits a fan-shaped sheet of fire 10 feet wide at its end and 12 feet long. Each creature touched \tab suffers six points of damage. The sheet of fire appears instantly, shoots forth dark red fla
mes, and snuffs out in less than one \tab second. It expends one charge.
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Pyrotechnics:}{\fs24  This function duplicates the spell of the same name. It has an initiative modifier of +2 and expends one charge.
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24  }{\i\fs24 Fireball:}{\fs24  The wand coughs forth a pea-sized sphere that streaks out to the desired range (to a maximum of 160 feet) and \tab bursts in a fiery, violet-red blast, just like the }{\i\fs24 fireball}{\fs24 
 spell. The initiative modifier is +2, and this expends two charges. The \tab fireball inflicts 6d6 points of damage, but all 1s rolled are counted as 2s (i.e., the burst causes 12-36 points). A saving throw \tab vs. wand is applicable.
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24  }{\i\fs24 Wall of fire:}{\fs24  The wand can be used to draw a fiery curtain of purplish-red flames 1200 feet square (10' x 120', 20' x \tab 60', 30' x 40', etc.). The flames la
st for six rounds and cause 2d6+6 points damage if touched (2d4 points if within 10 feet \tab of the fire, 1d4 if within 20 feet). The flames can also be shaped into a ring around the wand user (but the circle is 25 feet in \tab 
diameter). The initiative modifier is +3, and its use expends two charges.
\par \tab The }{\i\fs24 wand of fire}{\fs24  can operate just once per round. It can be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Flame Extinguishing:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This sort of wand has three separate functions:
\par \tab Nonmagical fires of normal size can be extinguished without using any charges. Normal size includes anything up to the size of a bonfire or a fire in a regular fireplace\emdash equal to four to six billets of wood burning hotly.
\par \tab To extinguish large, nonmagical fires, flaming oil in quantity equal to a gallon or more, the fire produced by a fiend, a }{\i\fs24 flame tongue}{\fs24  sword, or a }{\i\fs24 burning hands}{\fs24 
 spell, one charge is expended from the wand. Continual magical flames, such as those of a sword or a creature able to ignite, will be extinguished for six rounds and will flare up again after that time.
\par \tab When applied to large magical fires such as those caused by }{\i\fs24 fireball, flame strike,}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 wall of fire}{\fs24  spells, two charges are expended from the wand as the flames are extinguished.
\par \tab If the device is used upon a creature composed of flame (a fire elemental, for instance), a successful attack roll inflicts 6d6 points of damage upon the creature.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Frost:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A }{\i\fs24 frost}{\fs24  wand can perform three functions that duplicate wizard spells:
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24  }{\i\fs24 Ice storm:}{\fs24  A silvery ray springs forth from the wand and an ice (or sleet) storm occurs 
\par \tab up to 60 feet away from the wand holder. This function requires one charge.
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24  }{\i\fs24 Wall of ice:}{\fs24  The silvery ray forms a wall of ice, six inches thick, covering a 600-square-
\par \tab foot area (10' x 60', 20' x 30', etc.). Its initiative modifier is +2, and it uses one charge.
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24  }{\i\fs24 Cone of cold:}{\fs24  White crystalline motes spray forth from the wand in a cone with a 60-
\par \tab foot length and a terminal diameter of 20 feet. The initiative modifier is +2, and the 
\par \tab effect lasts just one second. The temperature is -100 degrees F., and damage is 
\par \tab 6d6, treating all 1s rolled as 2s (6d6, 12-36). The cost is two charges per use. Saving 
\par \tab throw vs. wands is applicable.
\par \tab The wand can function once per round, and may be recharged.
\par 
\par }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Illumination:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This wand has four separate functions, three of which approximate wizard spells, and one of which is unique:
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Dancing lights:}{\fs24  The wand produces this effect at a cost of one charge.
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Light: }{\fs24  The }{\i\fs24  illumination }{\fs24  wand sends forth light at an expenditure of one charge.
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Continual light: }{\fs24  This function require two charges.
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Sunburst:}{\fs24  When this effect is called forth, the wand delivers a sudden flash of brilliant, 
\par \tab greenish-white light, with blazing golden rays. The range of this sunburst is 120 yards 
\par \tab maximum, and its duration is 1/10 of a second. Its area of effect is a globe of 40-foot 
\par \tab diameter. Any undead within  this globe suffer 6d6 points of damage, with no saving 
\par \tab throw. Creatures within or facing the burst must roll successful saving throws vs. 
\par \tab wands or be blinded for one round and be unable to do anything during that period. 
\par \tab (Of course, the creatures in question  must have sight organs sensitive to the visible 
\par \tab light spectrum). The function requires three charges. 
\par \tab The wand can be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Illusion:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This wand creates audible and visual illusions (see }{\i\fs24 audible glamer, phantasmal force}{\fs24 ). The wand emits an invisible ray, with a 140-yard maximum range. T
he effect has an initiative modifier of +3. The wand wielder must concentrate on the illusion in order to maintain it\emdash 
he may move normally but can't melee during this time. Each portion, audible and visual, cost one charge to effect and one per round to continue. The wand may be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Lightning:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This wand has two functions that closely resemble wizard spells:
\par }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Shock:}{\fs24  This does 1-10 hit points of damage to a target struck in melee combat, with no 
\par \tab saving throw. Characters wearing metal armor and/or shields are treated as armor class 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\hich\af0\dbch\af0\loch\f0 10. \tab}}\pard \fi-360\li600\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls1\pnrnot0\pndec\pnb0\pni0\pnfs24\pnstart10\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta . }}\ls1\adjustright {\fs24 
Plain leather and wood work normally. Magical bonuses on metal armor do not 
\par }\pard \li240\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 affect Armor Class, but a }{\i\fs24 ring of protection}{\fs24  does. The shock uses one charge.
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\i\fs24 Lightning Bolt: }{\fs24  The possessor of the wand can discharge a bolt of lightning. The stroke 
\par \tab can be either a forked or straight bolt (see wizard spell,}{\i\fs24  lightning bolt}{\fs24 ). Damage is 12-
\par \tab 36 (6d6, treating 1s as 2s), but a saving throw is applicable. This function uses two 
\par \tab charges and has an initiate modifier of +2.
\par \tab The wand may be recharged. It can perform only one function per round.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Magic Detection:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This wand is similar in operation to the }{\i\fs24 enemy detection}{\fs24  wand. If any form of magic is in operation, or a magical item exists within a 30-foot radius, the }{\i\fs24 
magic detection}{\fs24  wand will pulse and point to the strongest source. Note that it will point to a person upon whom a spell has been cast.
\par \tab Operation requires one round, and successive rounds will point out successively
 less powerful magical radiation. The school of magic (abjuration, alteration, etc.) can be determined if one round is spent concentrating on the subject emanation. One charge is expended per turn (or fraction thereof) of use. Starting with the second rou
nd of continuous use, there is a 2% cumulative chance per round that the wand will temporarily malfunction and indicate nonmagical items as magical, or vice-versa. The wand may be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Magic Missiles:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This wand discharges magic missiles simi
lar to those of the 1st-level wizard spell of the same name. The missile causes 1d4+1 points of damage. It always hits its target when the wand is wielded by a wizard, otherwise an attack roll is required. The wand has an initiative modifier of +3, and ea
ch missile costs one charge. A maximum of two may be expended in one round. The wand may be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Metal and Mineral Detection:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This wand has a 30-foot radius range. It pulses in the wielder's hand and points to the largest mass of metal withi
n its effective area of operation. However, the wielder can concentrate on a specific metal or mineral (gold, platinum, quartz, beryl, diamond, corundum, etc.). If the specific mineral is within range, the wand will point to any and all places it is locat
ed, and the wand possessor will know the approximate quantity as well. Each operation requires one round. Each charge powers the wand for two full turns. The wand may be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Negation:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This device negates the spell or spell-like function(s) of rods, staves, wands, and other magical items. The individual with the }{\i\fs24  negation }{\fs24 
 wand points to the device, and a pale gray beam shoots forth to touch the target device or individual. This totally negates any wand function, and makes any other spell or 
spell-like function from that device 75% likely to be negated, regardless of the level or power of the spell. The wand can function once per round, and each negation drains one charge. The wand cannot be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Paralyzation: }{\fs24 This wand shoots 
forth a thin ray of bluish color to a maximum range of 60 feet. Any creature touched by the ray must roll successful saving throw vs. wand or be rendered rigidly immobile for 5d4 rounds. A save indicates the ray missed, and there is no effect. As soon as 
the ray touches one creature, it stops\emdash the wand can attack only one target per round. The wand has an initiative modifier of +3 , and each use costs one charge. The wand may operate once per round. It may be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Polymorphing:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This wand emits a thin, green beam that darts forth a maximum distance of 60 yards. Any creature touched by this beam must make a saving throw vs. wands (success indicating a miss) or be }{
\i\fs24  polymorphed}{\fs24  (as the }{\i\fs24 polymorph others}{\fs24  spell). The wielder may opt to turn the victim into a snail, frog, insect, etc., as long as the result is a small and inoffensive creature.
\par \tab The possessor of the wand may elect to touch a creature with the device instead. Unwilling creatures must be hit and are also entitled to a saving throw. If the
 touch is successful, the recipient is surrounded by dancing motes of sparkling emerald light, and then transforms into whatever creature-shape the wielder wants. This is the same magical effect as the }{\i\fs24 polymorph self}{\fs24  spell.
\par \tab Either function has an initiative modifier of +3. Each draws one charge. Only one function per round is possible. The wand may be recharged. 
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Secret Door and Trap Location:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This wand has an effective radius of 15 feet for secret door location and 30 feet for trap location. Whe
n the wand is energized it will pulse in the wielder's hand and point to all secret doors or traps within range. Note that it locates either doors }{\i\fs24  or }{\fs24 
 traps, not both during one operation. It requires one round to function and draws one charge. The wand may be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Size Alteration: }{\fs24 A wand of this sort enables the wielder to cause any single creature of virtually any size to }{\i\fs24  enlarge}{\fs24  or}{\i\fs24  diminish.}{\fs24  Either effect causes a 50% change in size.
\par \tab Relative Strength and power increases or decreases p
roportionally, providing the weaponry employed is proportionate or usable. For humanoid creatures enlarged, Strength is roughly proportional to that of a giant of corresponding size. For example, a humanoid enlarged to 9 feet tall is roughly equivalent to
 a hill giant (19 strength), and a 13-foot tall humanoid equals a fire giant (22 Strength).
\par \tab The wand's power has a range of 10 feet. The target creature and all it is wearing or carrying are affected unless a saving throw succeeds. Note that a willing target need not to make a saving throw.
\par \tab The effect of the wand can be removed by a }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  spell, but if this is done, the target must roll a system shock check. It can also be countered if the possessor of the wand wills the effect to be canceled before
 the duration of the effect expires. Each usage of the wand (but not the cancellation of an effect) expends one charge. It can be recharged by a wizard of 12th or higher level.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wand of Wonder:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The }{\i\fs24 wand of wonder}{\fs24  is a strange and unpre
dictable device that will generate any number of strange effects, randomly, each time it is used. The usual effects are shown on the table below, but you may alter these for any or all of these wands in your campaign as you see fit. Possible of the wand i
nclude:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx720\adjustright {\b\fs24 D100
\par }\pard\plain \s2\keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx720\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b {Roll\tab Effect
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx720\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 01-10\tab }{\i\fs24 Slow}{\fs24  creature pointed at for one turn
\par 11-18\tab }{\i\fs24 Deludes}{\fs24  wielder for one round into believing the wand functions as indicated by a 
\par \tab second die roll
\par 19-25\tab }{\i\fs24 Gust of wind,}{\fs24  double force of spell
\par 26-30\tab }{\i\fs24 Stinking cloud}{\fs24  at 30-foot  range
\par 31-33\tab }{\i\fs24 Heavy rain}{\fs24  falls for one round in 60-foot radius of wand wielder
\par 34-36\tab }{\i\fs24 Summon}{\fs24  rhino (1-25), elephant (26-50), or mouse (51-00)
\par 37-46\tab }{\i\fs24 Lightning bolt}{\fs24  (70' x 5') as wand
\par 47-49\tab Stream of 600 large butterflies pour forth and flutter around for two rounds, 
\par \tab blinding everyone (including wielder)
\par 50-53\tab }{\i\fs24 Enlarge}{\fs24  target if within 60 feet of wand
\par 54-58\tab }{\i\fs24 Darkness}{\fs24  in a 30-foot diameter hemisphere at 30 feet center distance from wand
\par 59-62\tab }{\i\fs24 Grass grows}{\fs24  in area of 160 square feet before the wand, or grass existing there 
\par \tab grows to 10 times normal size
