\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Bag of Devouring:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This bag appears to be an ordinary sack\emdash possibly appearing to be empty, possibly holding beans. The sack is, however, the lure used by an extradimensional creature\emdash 
this is one of its feeding orifices.
\par \tab Any substance of animal or vegetable nature is subject to "swallowing'' if it is thrust within the bag. The }{\i\fs24 bag of devouring}{\fs24 
 is 90% likely to ignore any initial intrusions, but any time it senses living human flesh within, it is 60% likely to close and attempt to draw the whole victim in\emdash base 75% chance for success, less Strength bonus for "damage,
'' and each +1 = -5% on base chance. Thus, an 18 Strength character (with +2 damage) is only 65% likely to be drawn into the bag, while a 5 Strength character (with -1 damage) is 80% likely to be drawn in.
\par \tab The bag radiates magic. It can hold up to 30 cubic feet of matter. It will act as a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24 
 (normal capacity), but each turn it has a 5% cumulative chance of "swallowing'' the contents and then "spitting the stuff out" in some non-space. Creatures drawn within are consumed in one round, eaten, and gone forever.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Bag of Holding:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 As with other magical bags, this one appears to be a common cloth sack of about 2 feet by 4 feet size. The }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24 
 opens into a nondimensional space, and its inside is larger than its outside dimensions. Regardless o
f what is put into this item, the bag always weighs a fixed amount. This weight, the bag's weight limit in contents, and its volume limit are determined by making a percentage roll and consulting the table below:
