\par \tab For example, on a roll of 9-12, the necklace will possess seven missiles\emdash one 7-dice, two 5-dice, and four 3-dice fireballs.
\par \tab The size will show that there is a difference in power between globes, but the number of dice and damage each causes cannot generally be known.
\par \tab If the necklace is being worn or carried by a character who fails his saving throw versus a magical fire attack, the item must undergo a saving throw check as well. If it fails to save, all remaining missiles detonate simultaneously.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Necklace of Prayer Beads}{\b\fs24 :}{\fs24  A magical necklace of this sort appears to be a normal piece of nonvaluable jewelry until it is placed about a character's neck. Even then, the true nature of the item will be
 revealed only if the wearer is a priest (excluding druids and characters otherwise able to use spells of a priestly or druidical nature such as paladins and rangers). The }{\i\fs24 necklace of prayer beads}{\fs24 
 consists of 1d6+24 semi-precious (60%) and fancy (40%) ston
es. The wearer will be 25% more likely to successfully petition his deity to grant desired spells. There will also be 1d4+2 special beads (precious stones, gems of 1,000 gp base value) of the following sort (roll 1d20 for each bead):
\par 
\par }\pard \ri-187\keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx180\tx1440\adjustright {\fs24\cf6 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 D20 Roll\tab Results}{\fs24\cf6 \tab 
\par }{\fs24 \tab 1-5\tab Bead of atonement\emdash as the 5th-level spell of the same name
\par \tab 6-10\tab Bead of blessing\emdash as the 1st-level spell of the same name
\par \tab 11-15\tab Bead of curing\emdash cures blindness, disease, or serious wounds (as the 
\par \tab \tab appropriate spells)
\par \tab 16-17\tab Bead of karma\emdash allows the priest to cast his spells as if he were four levels 
\par \tab \tab higher (with respect to range, duration, etc.)
\par \tab 18\tab Bead of summons\emdash calls the priest's deity (90% probability) to come to 
\par \tab \tab him in material form (but it had better be for a good reason!)
\par \tab 19-20\tab Bead of wind walking\emdash as the 7th-level spell of the same name
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Each special bead can be used once per day. If the priest summons his deity frivolously, the deity will, at the very least, take the necklace as punishment. The function of each bead is known only when the bead is grasped and a }{\i\fs24 commune}{
\fs24  spell used. All powers of the special beads are lost if they are removed from the necklace.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Necklace of Strangulation:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A }{\i\fs24 necklace of strangulation}{\fs24  can be identified only when placed around a character's neck. The necklac
e immediately constricts and cannot be removed by any means short of a }{\i\fs24 limited wish}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 
 spell. The wearer suffers 6 points of strangulation damage per round until he is dead. The necklace remains clasped around the character's throat until he is a dry skeleton. It can be reused.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Nolzur's Marvelous Pigments:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
These magical emulsions enable their possessor to create actual objects simply by depicting their form in two dimensions. The pigments are applied by a stick tipped with bristles, hair, or fur. The
 emulsion flows from the application to form the desired object as the wielder concentrates on the desired image. One pot of }{\i\fs24 Nolzur's marvelous pigments}{\fs24 
 is sufficient to create a 1,000-cubic-foot object by depicting it two-dimensionally over a 100-square-f
oot surface. Thus, a 10-foot by 10-foot rendition of a pit would result in an actual 10-foot by 10-foot by 10-foot pit; a 10-foot by 10-foot depiction of a room would result in a 10-foot by 10-foot by 10-foot room; and so on.
\par \tab Only normal, inanimate things can be created\emdash 
doors, pits, flowers, trees, cells, etc.; monsters, people, golems, and the like can't be created. The pigments must be applied to a surface (i.e., a floor, wall, ceiling, door, etc.). From 1d4 containers of pigments will be found, usually 
with a single instrument about 1 foot long with which to apply them. It takes one turn to depict an object with pigments. Objects of value depicted by pigments\emdash precious metals, gems, jewelry, ivory, etc.\emdash 
will appear to be valuable but will really be made of tin, lead, paste gems, brass, bone, etc. Normal armor or weapons can, of course, be created.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pearl of Power:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This seemingly normal pearl of average size and coloration is a potent aid to a wizard. Once a day, a }{\i\fs24 pearl of power}{\fs24  enables the possessor to r
ecall any one spell as desired, even if the spell has already been cast. Of course, the wizard must have the spell to be remembered among those he most recently memorized. The power of the pearl is determined by rolling percentile dice and consulting the 
table below:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx720\tx1440\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 D100\tab    Level of Spell\tab 
\par Roll\tab    Recalled by Pearl}{\fs24\cf6 \tab 
\par }{\fs24 01-25\tab \tab first\tab 
\par 26-45\tab \tab second\tab 
\par 46-60\tab \tab third\tab 
\par 61-75\tab \tab fourth\tab 
\par 76-85\tab \tab fifth\tab 
\par 86-92\tab \tab sixth\tab 
\par 93-96\tab \tab seventh\tab 
\par 97-98\tab \tab eighth\tab 
\par 99\tab \tab ninth\tab 
\par 00\tab \tab recalls two spells of 1st to 6th level (use 1d6)
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab One in 20 of these pearls is of opposite effect, causing a spell to be forgotten. These pearls can be gotten rid of only by means of a }{\i\fs24 wish!}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pearl of the Sirines:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This normal-seeming pearl radiates faintly of enchantment if magic is detected for. In any 
event, the stone will be very beautiful and worth at least 1,000 gp on this basis alone. If it is clasped firmly in hand (or to the breast) and the possessor attempts actions related to the pearl's power areas, he will understand and be able to employ the
 item.
\par \tab The pearl enables its possessor to breathe in water as if he were in clean, fresh air. Underwater movement rate is 24. The possessor is immune to ill effects from the poison touch of a sirine. The pearl must be within the general area of the possess
or\emdash less than 10 feet distant\emdash to convey its powers to him.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pearl of Wisdom:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Although it appears to be a normal pearl, a }{\i\fs24 pearl of wisdom}{\fs24  causes a priest to increase one point in Wisdom if he retains the pearl for one month. The increase happens at the end 
of 30 days, but thereafter the priest must keep the pearl with him or the one point gain will be lost.
\par \tab Note that one in 20 of these magical pearls is cursed to work in reverse, but once the point of Wisdom is lost, the pearl turns to powder; the loss is permanent barring some magical restoration means such as a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 
tome of understanding.}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Periapt of Foul Rotting:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This engraved gem appears to be a gem of small value. If any character claims it as his own, he will contract a terrible rotting disease which can be removed only by application of a }{
\i\fs24 remove curse}{\fs24  spell followed by a }{\i\fs24 cure disease}{\fs24  and then a }{\i\fs24 heal, limited wish}{\fs24 , or }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  spell. The rotting can also be countered by crushing a }{\i\fs24 periapt of health}{\fs24 
 and sprinkling its dust upon the afflicted character. O
therwise, the afflicted loses one point each of Dexterity, Constitution, and Charisma per week beginning one week after claiming the item. When any score reaches 0, the character is dead. Each point lost due to the disease will be permanent regardless of 
subsequent removal of the affliction.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Periapt of Health:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This gem appears exactly the same as a }{\i\fs24 periapt of foul rotting,}{\fs24 
 but the possessor will be immune from all diseases save that of the latter periapt so long as he has it on his person.
\par 
\par }{\fs24\cf6 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Periapt of Proof Against Poison:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 The }{\i\fs24 periapt of proof against poison}{\fs24 
 is indistinguishable from other periapts. The character who has one of these magical gems is allowed a saving throw vs. poison that normally disallow any such opportunity. The Special Save column on t
he table below lists the saving throw for such poisons. The owner rolls against his normal score for poisons which are usually at a penalty, and gets a plus on all other poison saves. Roll 1d20 and consult the table below to determine the effectiveness of
 a particular periapt:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s7\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\tx2880\outlinelevel6\adjustright \b\cf6 {D20\tab Special\tab Plus of\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\tx2880\adjustright \fs20 {\b\fs24\cf6 Roll\tab Save\tab Periapt}{\fs24\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 1-8\tab 19\tab +1\tab 
\par 9-14\tab 17\tab +2\tab 
\par 15-18\tab 15\tab +3\tab 
\par 19-20\tab 13\tab +4\tab 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Periapt of Wound Closure:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This magical stone looks exactly the same as the others of this il
k. The person possessing it need never fear open, bleeding wounds because the periapt prevents them. In addition, the periapt doubles the normal rate of healing, or allows normal healing of wounds that would not do so normally.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Philosopher's Stone:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This 
is a rare and magical substance that appears to be an ordinary, sooty black piece of rock. It radiates faintly of unknown magic. If the stone is broken open, a cavity will be discovered. The interior of this cavity is lined with a quicksilver which enable
s the transmutation of the base metals (iron and lead) into silver and gold. A wizard will be required to bring about such transmutation, however.
\par \tab From 50 to 500 pounds of iron can be made into silver; from 10 to 100 pounds of lead can be turned into gold from a single }{\i\fs24 philosopher's stone}{\fs24 
. Transmutation must be made fully upon the first attempt, all the quicksilver being employed at one time.
\par \tab Two other substances may be found in a }{\i\fs24 philosopher's stone}{\fs24  instead of the quicksilver described above, at the DM's 
discretion. If he decides there's something different in the stone, there is a 75% chance that a greenish, crystalline salt will be found. This allows the manufacture of 1d4 }{\i\fs24 potions of longevity.}{\fs24 
 There is a 25% chance that a white powder will be found in a stone. When mixed with a }{\i\fs24 potion of longevity,}{\fs24 
 this can actually restore life to a dead human or demihuman. The mixture must be administered internally within one week of the creature's demise (see the }{\i\fs24 raise dead}{\fs24  spell).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Phlactery of Faithfulness:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 There is no means to determine what function this device performs until it is worn. The wearer of a }{\i\fs24 phylactery of faithfulness}{\fs24 
 will be aware of any action or item that will adversely affect his alignment and standing with his deity. He acquires this inform
ation prior to performing the action or becoming associated with such an item, if a moment is taken to contemplate the action. The phylactery must be worn normally by the priest, of course.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Phylactery of Long Years:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This device slows the aging process by
 one-quarter for as long as the priest wears it. The reduction applies even to magical aging. Thus, if a priest dons the phylactery at age 20, he will age nine months in every 12 that pass; in 12 chronological years, he will have aged just nine years, and
 will be 29 (physically) rather than 32. One in 20 of these devices is cursed to operate in reverse.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Phylactery of Monstrous Attention:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 While this arm wrapping appears to be a beneficial device, it actually draws the attention of supernatural creatures of
 exactly the opposite alignment of the priest wearing it. This results in the priest being plagued by powerful and hostile creatures whenever he is in an area where such creatures are or can appear. If the priest is of 10th or higher level, the attention 
of his deity's most powerful enemy will be drawn, causing this being to interfere directly. Once donned, a }{\i\fs24 phylactery of monstrous attention}{\fs24  cannot be removed without a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24 
 spell and then a quest must be performed to re-establish the priest in his alignment.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pipes of Haunting:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
This magical item appears to be a small set of pan pipes. If checked, it faintly radiates magic. When played by a person skilled in music, the pipes create an eerie, spell-binding tune. A listener will think the source of the music 
is somewhere within 30 feet of the musician. Those hearing the tune and not aware of the piper must make a saving throw vs. spell. Those who fail become nervous and scared. All morale checks are made with a -2 penalty and the listeners suffer a -1 penalty
 to all surprise rolls.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pipes of Pain:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
These appear to be like any other standard or magical set of pipes with nothing to reveal their true nature. When played by a character proficient in music, the pipes create a wondrous melody, surpassing any sound ev
er heard. All within 30 feet, including the piper, must save vs. spells or be enchanted by the sound. So long as the pipes are played, no one will attack or attempt any action if affected.
\par \tab As soon as the piping stops, all those affected will be stricken b
y intense pain at even the slightest noise, causing 1d4 points of damage per round. This pain will last for 2d4 rounds. Thereafter, the least noise will cause the victim to wince, reducing the character's attack and saving throw rolls -2. The effect can b
e negated only by a }{\i\fs24 forget}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 remove curse}{\fs24  spell.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pipes of Sounding:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 When played by a character proficient in music, these pipes can be used to create a variety of sounds. To a listener the source of the sound will seem to be anywhere within 60 feet of th
e piper. The possible sounds that can be created are: wind blowing, laughter, whistling, bird calls, moaning, footsteps, crying, mumbled voices, screams, running water, or creaking. (Note: The DM can rule that other similar sounds are possible.)
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pipes of the Sewers:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
These wooden pipes appear ordinary, but if the possessor learns the proper tune, he can attract from 10-60 (1d6 x 10) giant rats (80%) or 30-180 (3d6 x 10) normal rats (20%) if either or both are within 400 feet. For each 50-foot distance the 
rats have to travel, there will be a one-round delay. The piper must continue playing until the rats appear, and when they do so, they are 95% likely to obey the piper so long as he continues to play. If for any reason the piper ceases playing, the rats s
ummoned will leave immediately. If they are called again, it is 70% probable that they will come and obey, 30% likely that they will turn upon the piper.
\par \tab If the rats are under control of a creature such as a vampire, the piper's chance of taking over contr
ol is 30% per round of piping. Once control is assumed, there is a 70% chance of maintaining it if the other creature is actively seeking to reassert its control.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Portable Hole:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A portable hole is a circle of magical cloth spun from the webs of a phase s
pider interwoven with strands of ether and beams of Astral plane luminaries. When opened fully, a portable hole is 6 feet in diameter, but it can be folded as small as a pocket handkerchief. When spread upon any surface, it causes an extra-dimensional hol
e 10 feet deep to come into being. This hole can be "picked up" from inside or out by simply taking hold of the edges of the magical cloth and folding it up. Either way, the entrance disappears, but anything inside the hole remains.
\par \tab 
The only oxygen in the hole is that allowed by creation of the space, so creatures requiring the gas cannot remain inside for more than a turn or so without opening the space again by means of the magical cloth. The cloth does not accumulate weight even i
f its hole is filled (with gold, for example). Each }{\i\fs24 portable hole}{\fs24  opens on its own particular nondimensional space. If a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  is placed within a }{\i\fs24 portable hole,}{\fs24 
 a rift to the Astral Plane is torn in the space, and the bag and the cloth are sucked into the void and forever lost. If a }{\i\fs24 portable hole}{\fs24  is placed within a }{\i\fs24 bag of holding,}{\fs24 
 it opens a gate to another plane, and the hole, bag, and any creatures within a 10-foot radius are drawn to the Astral Plane, the }{\i\fs24 portable hole}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 bag of holding}{\fs24  being destroyed in the process.
\par \tab 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Pouch of Accessibility:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
This normal-seeming pouch is actually a strongly magicked item which can contain up to 300 lbs. in 30 specially constructed pockets within it. Each pocket, in turn, holds a maximum of 10 lbs., or one cubic foot of volume, whichever is reached first.

\par \tab This device also enables the possessor to open it and call forth the item(s) desired. Merely speaking the name of a desired object causes it to appear at the top of the pouch, ready for instant grasp.
\par \tab These items are similar to }{\i\fs24 bags of holding}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 portable holes,}{\fs24  and the strictures about placement within such magical spaces apply fully. The pouch weighs 1 lb. empty and 4 lbs. when filled.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Quaal's Feather Token:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\i\fs24 Feather tokens}{\fs24  are small magical devices of vari
ous forms to suit special needs. The types of tokens are listed below. Other token-types can be created as desired. Each token is usable once. To determine the type of }{\i\fs24 feather token}{\fs24  discovered, consult the following table:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 D20 Roll\tab Tokens}{\fs24\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 1-4\tab Anchor
\par 5-7\tab Bird
\par 8-10\tab Fan
\par 11-13\tab Swan Boat
\par 14-18\tab Tree
\par 19-20\tab Whip
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Anchor:}{\fs24  a token useful to moor a craft in water so as to render it immobile for up to one full day.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Bird:}{\fs24  a token that can be used to drive off hostile avian creatures or as a vehicle of transportation equal to a roc of the largest size (one-day duration).
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Fan:}{\fs24  a token that forms a huge flapping fan which can cause a strong breeze in an area large enough to propel one ship. This wind is not cumulative with existing wind speeds\emdash if there is already a stro
ng breeze blowing, this cannot be added to it to create a gale. It can, however, be used against it to create an area of relative calm or lesser winds (though this will not affect wave size in a storm, of course). The fan can be used up to eight hours a d
ay. It will not function on land.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Swan boat:}{\fs24  a token that forms a huge swanlike boat capable of swimming at a rate of 24, and carrying eight horses and gear or 32 men or any equivalent combination (one-day duration).
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Tree:}{\fs24  a token that causes a great oak to spring into being (6-foot diameter trunk, 60-foot height, 40-foot top diameter).
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Whip:}{\fs24  a token that causes a huge leather whip to appear and be wielded against any opponent desired (+1 weapon, 9th-level fighter's attack roll, 1d6+1 points damage plus a
 saving throw vs. spell or be bound fast for 1d6+1 rounds) for up to six turns. (See }{\i\fs24 Sword of dancing}{\fs24 ).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Quiver of Ehlonna:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This appears to be a typical arrow container capable of holding about 20 arrows. It has an aura of alteration if magic is detected 
for, and examination shows that it has three distinct portions. The first and smallest one can contain up to 60 objects of the same general size and shape as long bow arrows. The second, slightly longer, compartment will hold up to 18 objects of the same 
general size and shape as a javelin. The third and longest portion of the case will contain as many as six objects of the same general size and shape as a bow\emdash 
spears or staves, for example. Such a quiver is always found empty, but once the owner has filled it, he can command the quiver to produce any stored items he wishes each round.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Robe of the Archmagi:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This normal-appearing garment can be white (45%\emdash good alignment), gray (30%\emdash neutral, but neither good nor evil, alignment), or black (25%\emdash evil alignment)
. Its wearer gains the following powers:
\par 
\par \tab 1. It serves as armor equal to AC 5.
\par \tab 2. The robe confers a 5% magic resistance.
\par \tab 3. It adds a +1 bonus to saving throw scores.
\par \tab 4. The robe reduces the victim's magic resistance and saving throws by 20%/-4 when the wearer casts any of the following spells: }{\i\fs24 charm monster, charm person, friends, hold monster, hold person, polymorph other, suggestion.}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab The color of a }{\i\fs24 robe of the archmagi}{\fs24 
 is not determined until it is donned by a wizard. If a white robe is donned by an evil wizard, he suffers 11d4+7 points of damage and loses 18,000-51,000 experience points at the DM's discretion. The reverse is true with respect to a black robe donned
 by a good aligned wizard. An evil or good wizard putting on a gray robe, or a neutral wizard donning either a white or black robe, incurs 6d4 points damage, 6,000-24,000 experience points loss, and the wearer will be moved toward the alignment of the rob
e by its enchantments (i.e., he will feel himself urged to change alignment to that of the robe, and he will have to make an effort to maintain his old alignment).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Robe of Blending:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This ordinary-appearing robe cannot be detected by magical means. When it is put on, however, the wearer will know that the garment has very special properties. A }{\i\fs24 robe of blending}{\fs24 
 enables its wearer to appear to be part of a rock wall or a plant\emdash whatever is appropriate. It can even make the wearer appear to be a creature of his choice.
\par \tab The robe does have its limits: It will not make its wearer appear to be more than twice normal height or less than one-half normal. It does not impart vocal capabilities\emdash 
either understanding or imitating the creature the wearer looks like. (In situations where several different forms are appropriate, the wearer is obliged to state which form he wishes the robe to camouflage him as.)
\par \tab Creatures with exceptional (15+) or better Intelligence have a 1% per Intelligence point chance of detecting something amiss when they are within 30 feet of someone disguising himself with a }{\i\fs24 robe of blending.}{\fs24 
 Creatures with low Intelligence or better and 10 or more levels of experience or Hit Dice have a 1% chance per level or Hit Die of likewise noting something unusu
al about a robe-wearing character. (The latter is cumulative with the former chance for detection, so an 18 Intelligence wizard of 12th level has a 30% chance\emdash 18% + 12%\emdash 
of noting something amiss.) After an initial check per eligible creature, successive ch
ecks should be made each turn thereafter, if the same creatures are within the 30-foot range. All creatures acquainted with and friendly to the wearer will see him normally.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Robe of Eyes:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
This valuable garment appears to be a normal robe until it is put on. Its wearer is able to "see'' in all directions at the same moment due to scores of magical "eyes'' which adorn the robe. The wearer also gains infravision to a range of 120 feet, and th
e power to see displaced or out-of-phase objects and creatures in their actual positions. The }{\i\fs24 robe of eyes}{\fs24  sees all forms of invisible things within a 240-foot normal vision range (or 120 feet if }{\i\fs24 infravision}{\fs24 
 is being used).
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Invisibility, dust of disappearance, robes of blending,}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 improved invisibility}{\fs24 
 are not proof against observation, but astral or ethereal things cannot be seen by means of this robe. Solid objects obstruct even the robe's powers of observation. Illusions and secret doors also can't be 
seen, but creatures camouflaged or hidden in shadows are easily detected, so ambush or surprise of a character wearing a }{\i\fs24 robe of eyes}{\fs24  is impossible.
\par \tab Finally, the robe enables its wearer to track as if he were a 12th-level ranger. A }{\i\fs24 light}{\fs24  spell thrown directly on a }{\i\fs24 robe of eyes}{\fs24  will blind it for 1d3 rounds, a }{\i\fs24 continual light}{\fs24  for 2d4 rounds.

\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Robe of Powerlessness:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A }{\i\fs24 robe of powerlessness}{\fs24  appears to be a robe of another sort, and detection will discover nothing more than the fact that it has a magical aura.
 As soon as a character dons this garment, he drops to 3 Strength and 3 Intelligence, forgetting all spells and magical knowledge. The robe can be removed easily, but in order to restore mind and body, the character must have a }{\i\fs24 remove curse}{
\fs24  spell and then a }{\i\fs24 heal}{\fs24  spell placed upon him.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Robe of Scintillating Colors:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This garment appears quite normal, but a magical aura is detectable. Only a wearer with an Intelligence of 15 or higher and a Wisdom of 13 or more can cause a }{\i\fs24 
robe of scintillating colors}{\fs24  to fun
ction. If Intelligence and Wisdom are sufficient, the wearer can cause the garment to become a shifting pattern of incredible hues, color after color cascading from the upper part of the robe to the hem in sparkling rainbows of dazzling light.
\par \tab This effect
 sheds light in a 40-foot diameter sphere, and it has the power to hypnotize opponents, making them unable to attack the wearer. A full round passes before the colors begin "flowing'' on the robe. Each round after that, any opponent who fails a saving thr
ow vs. spell (or magic resistance check, then save) will stand hypnotized and transfixed for 1d4+1 rounds. Even when this effect wears off, additional saves must be made in order to attack.
\par \tab Furthermore, every round of continuous scintillation of the robe makes the wearer 5% more difficult to hit with missile attacks or hand-held or body weaponry (hands, fists, claws, fangs, horns, etc.) until a maximum of 25% (-5) is attained\emdash 
five continuous rounds of the dazzling play of hues.
\par \tab After the initial round of c
oncealment, the wearer is able to cast spells or engage in all forms of activity that do not require movement of more than 10 feet from his starting position. In noncombat situations, the robe simply hypnotizes creatures failing their saving throws vs. sp
ell for 1d4+1 turns.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Robe of Stars:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
This rather ordinary-appearing garment seems typical of apparel worn by a wizard. However, it will radiate a strong aura of alteration and evocation if examined for magic. The robe enables its wearer to travel physicall
y on the Astral Plane, along with all that he is wearing or carrying. The garment also enables the wearer to survive comfortably in the void of outer space. In other situations, the robe gives its wearer a +1 bonus to all saving throws.
\par \tab The robe is embroi
dered with stars, and the wearer can use up to six of these as missile weapons, provided he is proficient with darts as a weapon. Each star is a throwing weapon of +5 value, both to hit and damage. Maximum range is 60 feet and base damage is 2d4 points pe
r
 hit. The special star weapons are located on the chest portion of the robe. If the wearer does not use all of these missiles, they will replace themselves magically at the rate of one per day. If all six are used, all of the robe's traveling and missile 
powers are gone forever.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Robe of Useful Items:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
This appears to be an unremarkable item of apparel, but a wizard who dons it will note that it is adorned with small cloth patches of various shapes. Only the wearer of the robe can see, recognize, and detach
 these patches. One patch can be detached each round. Detaching a patch causes it to become an actual item, as indicated below. A }{\i\fs24 robe of useful items}{\fs24  always begins with two each of the following patches:
\par \tab   dagger
\par \tab   lantern (filled and lit)
\par \tab   mirror (large)
\par \tab   pole (10-foot length)
\par \tab   rope (50-foot coil)
\par \tab   sack (large)
\par 
\par \tab In addition, the robe will have 4d4 items which must be diced for. Roll 4d4 to determine how many additional items a robe has and then percentile dice to determine specific items:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 D100 Roll\tab Result}{\fs24\cf6 
\par }{\fs24 01-08\tab Bag of 100 gold pieces
\par 09-15\tab Coffer (\'bd' x \'bd' x 1'), silver (500 gp value)
\par 16-22\tab Door, iron (up to 10 feet wide and 10 feet high and barred on 1 side\emdash must
\par \tab be placed upright, will attach and hinge itself)
\par 23-30\tab Gems, 10 of 100 gp value each
\par 31-44\tab Ladder, wooden (24 feet long)
\par 45-51\tab Mule (with saddle bags)
\par 52-59\tab Pit (10 cubic feet), open
\par 60-68\tab }{\i\fs24 Potion of extra healing}{\fs24 
\par 69-75\tab Rowboat (12 feet long)
\par 76-83\tab Scroll of one randomly determined spell
\par 84-90\tab War dogs, pair
\par 91-96\tab Window (2 feet by 4 feet\emdash up to 2 feet deep)
\par 97-00\tab Roll twice more
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab Multiple items of the same kind are permissible. Once removed, items are never replaced.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Robe of Vermin:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This magical-seeming garment will radiate a dim aura of enchantment if magic is detected for
. The wearer will notice nothing unusual when the robe is donned, and it will actually convey some magical power at that time\emdash }{\i\fs24 protection +1,}{\fs24 
 for example. However, as soon as the wearer is in a situation requiring concentration and action against hostile op
ponents, the true nature of the garment will be revealed: The wearer immediately suffers a multitude of bites from the insects that magically infest the garment. He must cease all other activities in order to scratch, shift the robe, and generally show si
gns of extreme discomfort from the movement and biting of these pests.
\par \tab The wearer is unable to gain initiative, and has a 50% chance of being unable to complete a spell due to the vermin. All other actions and attack forms requiring manual/locomotive/somat
ic activity are at half normal probability. The garment can't be removed except by means of a }{\i\fs24 remove curse}{\fs24  spell or similar magic.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rope of Climbing:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A 60-foot long }{\i\fs24 rope of climbing}{\fs24 
 is no thicker than a slender wand and weighs no more than three pounds, but it is strong enough to support 3,000 pounds. Upon command, the rope will snake forward, upward, downward, or any other direction at 10 feet per round and attach itself securely w
herever desired. It will return or unfasten itself in a similar manner. A }{\i\fs24 rope of climbing}{\fs24 
 can also be commanded to knot itself. This causes large knots to appear at 1-foot intervals along the rope. Knotting shortens the rope to a 50-foot length until the k
nots are untied. One end of the rope must be held by a character when its magic is invoked.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rope of Constriction:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This rope looks exactly like a }{\i\fs24 rope of climbing}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 entanglement.}{\fs24  As soon as it is commanded to perform some action, however, it lashes itsel
f about the neck of the character holding it, and from 1d4 others within 10 feet. Everyone caught by the rope is entitled to a saving throw vs. spell. Anyone failing the saving throw is strangled and crushed (2d6 hit points of damage), and the rope contin
ues to constrict until a }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  is cast upon it.
\par \tab Creatures entwined by the rope cannot cast spells or free themselves. An unentangled character can cast a }{\i\fs24 dispel magic}{\fs24  or try to cut through the rope\emdash it is AC -2 and takes 22 points of damage to cut th
rough; all hit points must be inflicted by the same creature (not the one entangled).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rope of Entanglement:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A }{\i\fs24 rope of entanglement}{\fs24  looks just like any other magical rope. Upon command, the rope lashes forward 20 feet or upward 10 feet to entangle up to e
ight man-sized creatures. For purposes of entanglement, creatures of different sizes are assigned values, as follows:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s7\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\outlinelevel6\adjustright \b\cf6 {Size\tab Value
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 Tiny\tab .33*\tab 
\par Small\tab .5*\tab 
\par Medium\tab 1\tab 
\par Large\tab 3\tab 
\par Huge\tab 4\tab 
\par Gigantic\tab 8
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 * Round up.
\par 
\par \tab Add the values of all creatures entangled to determi
ne how many are affected by the rope. For example, the rope could entangle up to 24 Tiny creatures or 2 Huge creatures. Any combination of sizes is possible as long as the total value doesn't exceed eight.
\par \tab The rope cannot be broken by sheer strength\emdash it mu
st be hit by an edged weapon. The rope is AC -2 and takes 22 points of damage to cut through; all damage must be inflicted by the same creature (not the one entangled). Damage under 22 points will repair itself in six turns. If a }{\i\fs24 
rope of entanglement}{\fs24  is severed, it is destroyed.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rug of Smothering:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This finely woven carpet resembles a }{\i\fs24 carpet of flying}{\fs24  and will detect as magical. The character seating himself upon it and giving a command will be surprised, however, as the }{
\i\fs24 rug of smothering}{\fs24  rolls itself tigh
tly around him, suffocating him in 1d4+2 rounds. The rug cannot be physically prevented from wrapping itself, and it can be prevented from smothering its victim only by the casting of any one of the following spells: }{\i\fs24 
animate object, hold plant, wish.}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Rug of Welcome:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A rug of this type appears exactly the same as a }{\i\fs24 carpet of flying,}{\fs24  and it performs the functions of one (6-foot by 9-foot size), but a }{\i\fs24 rug of welcome}{\fs24 
 has other, additional powers. Upon command it will function as a }{\i\fs24 rug of smothering,}{\fs24  entrapping any creature up to ogre-size which steps upon it. A }{\i\fs24 rug of welcome}{\fs24 
 can also elongate itself and become as hard and strong as steel, the maximum length being 27 feet by 2 feet. In this form, it can serve as a bridge, barricade, etc. In this latt
er form it is AC 0 and will take 100 points of damage to destroy. Finally, the possessor need only utter a word of command, and the rug will shrink to half size for easy storage and transportation.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Saw of Might Cutting:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This notched adamantite blade is 1
2 feet long and over 1 foot wide. It requires 18/00 or greater Strength to operate alone, or two people of 17 or greater Strength working in tandem. The blade will slice through a 1-foot diameter tree in three rounds, a 2-foot thick hardwood tree in one t
urn, or a 4-foot thick trunk in three turns. After six turns (cumulative) of cutting with the saw, the character or characters must rest for six turns before doing any further work.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scarab of Death:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This small pin appears to be any one of the various bene
ficial amulets, brooches, or scarabs. However, if it is held for more than one round or placed within a soft container (bag, pack, etc.) within 1 foot of a warm, living body for one turn, it changes into a horrible burrowing beetle-like creature. The thin
g
 will tear through any leather or cloth, burrow into flesh, and reach the victim's heart in a single round, causing death. It then returns to its scarab form. (Placing the scarab in a container of hard wood, ceramic, bone, ivory, or metal will prevent the
 monster from coming to life.)
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scarab of Enraging Enemies:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 When one of these devices is displayed and a command uttered, all intell
igent hostile creatures within a 40-foot radius must successfully save vs. spell or become enraged. Those whose saving throws succeed may perform normally; enraged enemies fly into a berserk fury and attack the nearest creature, even their own comrades (+
1 bonus to attack rolls, +2 bonus to damage, -3 to their own Armor Class).
\par \tab The rage lasts for 1d6+6 rounds, and during this period, the enraged creatures will attack continually, without reason or fear, moving on to attack other creatures nearest them if i
nitial opponents are slain. A scarab of this type contains from 1d6+18 charges.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scarab of Insanity:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This item is indistinguishable from any other amulet, brooch, or scarab. When displayed and a command word is spoken, all other creatures within a 20-foot
 radius must save vs. spell with a -2 penalty (and -10% penalty to any magic resistance as well). Those failing the save are completely insane for 1d4+8 rounds, unable to cast spells or use reasoning of any sort (treat as a }{\i\fs24 confusion spell}{
\fs24  with no chance for acting in a non-confused manner). The scarab has 1d8+8 charges.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scarab of Protection:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This device appears to be any one of the various magical amulets, stones, etc. It gives off a faint magical aura, however, and if it is held for one round, an inscri
ption will appear on its surface letting the holder know it is a protective device.
\par \tab The possessor gains a +1 bonus to all saving throws vs. spell. If no save is normally possible, he gets a one in 20 chance of saving, adjusted by any other magical protect
ions that normally give bonuses to saving throws. Thus, this device allows a saving throw vs. spell at base 20 against }{\i\fs24 magic missile}{\fs24  attacks, for example. If the target also has a +4 bonus for magical armor and a +1 bonus for a }{
\i\fs24 ring of protection,}{\fs24  any roll of 15 or better would indicate that the missiles did no damage.
\par \tab The scarab can also absorb up to 12 level-draining attacks (two level drains count as two absorbings), }{\i\fs24 death touches, death rays,}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 fingers of death.}{\fs24  However, upon absorbing 12 such attacks
 the scarab turns to powder\emdash totally destroyed.
\par \tab One in 20 of these scarabs will be a cursed item, giving the possessor a -2 penalty to his saving throws. However, one in five of these cursed items will become a +2 scarab if the curse is removed by a cleric
 of 16th-level or higher. In this case, the scarab will have absorption capability of 24 rather than 12.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Scarab Versus Golems:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This magical pin enables its wearer to detect any golem within 60 feet, although he must concentrate in order for the detection
 to take place. Furthermore, the scarab enables its possessor to combat a golem, with hand-held or missile weapons, as if it were a normal monster, with no special defenses. Each scarab has this effect with regard to a different sort of golem. Roll percen
tile die and consult the table below:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s7\keep\keepn\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\tx3600\outlinelevel6\adjustright \b\cf6 {D100\tab Type(s) of Golem\tab XP\tab 
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\tx3600\adjustright \fs20 {\b\fs24\cf6 Roll\tab Affected by Scarab\tab Value\tab }{\fs24\cf6  
\par }{\fs24 01-30\tab Flesh\tab    400\tab 
\par 31-55\tab Clay\tab    500\tab 
\par 56-75\tab Stone\tab    600\tab 
\par 76-85\tab Iron\tab    800\tab 
\par 86-95\tab Flesh, Clay, Wood\tab    900\tab 
\par 96-00\tab Any golem\tab 1,250\tab 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Sheet of Smallness:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A magical item of this sort appears to be nothing more than a well-made piece of material\emdash 
possibly some sort of covering or sheet woven of very fine linen or silk. One side will have a larger pattern than the other, or perhaps one side will be white, the other 
black. In any event, there will be an aura of alteration detectable from this cloth if magic is checked for.
\par \tab This item causes any magical item wrapped within it to shrink to 1/12 its normal size and weight. If the item is then wrapped in the sheet so as t
o be touching the reverse side of the material, it will grow back to its normal size and weight. Note that this item has no effect on artifacts, relics, or living material\emdash it affects only non-living, ordinary magical items\emdash 
and no item shrunk in this fashion is functional or usable while in reduced form. Change in size requires two rounds to accomplish, either in shrinking or restoring to normal size.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Slippers of Spider Climbing:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 These shoes appear unremarkable, although they will give off a faint aura of 
alteration magic if detected for. When worn, a pair of these slippers enable the individual to move at a 60-foot rate on vertical surfaces or even upside down along ceilings, with hands free to do whatever the wearer desires. Extremely slippery surfaces
\emdash ice, oiled, or greased surfaces\emdash make these slippers useless.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Smoke Powder:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This magical substance is similar, though not identical, to gunpowder. It is extremely scarce and, due to its volatile nature, dangerous to fabricate. }{\i\fs24 Smoke powder}{\fs24 
 will be available in a campaign only if the DM allows it. If the DM doesn't want it in the campaign, it simply doesn't exist.
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Smoke powder}{\fs24  is commonly found divided into two separate components\emdash one, a steely-blue granular substance, the other, a fine whi
te powder. Alone, each component is inert and harmless. However, when equal portions of the two are mixed together, the }{\i\fs24 smoke powder}{\fs24  is complete and dangerous.
\par \tab When touched by a flame, the mixed powder explodes with great force, noise, and smoke. The size and force of the explosion varies according to the amount of }{\i\fs24 smoke powder}{\fs24 
 used. A small, measured amount (a spoonful of each component) causes 1d2 points of damage. Such an amount is sufficient for a large firecracker or a single charge of an arquebus (if
 these optional weapons exist in the campaign). Increasing the amount increases the damage proportionally\emdash doubling causes 2d2 points of damage, tripling causes 3d2, and so on.
\par \tab An explosion capable of causing 30 points of damage (15 charges) has a 5-foot radius. Blasts capable of causing 50 or more points of damage (25 or more charges) have a radius of 15 feet, and affect items and fortifications as would a giant's blow.

\par \tab When discovered, a pouch of }{\i\fs24 smoke powder}{\fs24  contains 3d6 charges. Charges from several pouches of }{\i\fs24 smoke powder}{\fs24  can be combined to create bigger, more damaging explosions.
\par \tab 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Sovereign Glue:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This pale amber substance is thick and viscous. Because of its particular powers, it can be contained only within a flask coated with }{\i\fs24 oil of slipperiness,}{\fs24  
and each time any of the bonding agent is poured from the flask, a new application of the }{\i\fs24 oil of slipperiness}{\fs24  must be put on the flask within one round to prevent the remaining glue from adhering to the side of the container.
\par \tab One ounce of the adhesive wil
l cover approximately one square foot of surface, bonding virtually any two substances together in a permanent union. The glue takes one full round to set; if the objects are pulled apart before that time has elapsed, that application of the glue will los
e its stickiness and be worthless. If the glue is allowed to set, then attempting to separate the two bonded objects will only result in the rending of one or the other except when }{\i\fs24 oil of etherealness}{\fs24  or }{\i\fs24 universal solvent}{
\fs24  is applied to the bond\emdash }{\i\fs24 sovereign glue}{\fs24  is dissolved only by those liquids. A typical container of the substance holds 1d10 ounces of glue.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Spade of Colossal Excavation:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This digging tool is 8 feet long with a spade-like blade 2 feet wide and 3 feet long. Any fighter with 18 Strength can us
e this magical shovel to dig great holes. One cubic yard of normal earth can be excavated in one round. After 10 rounds of digging, the user must rest for five rounds. Hard pan clay takes twice as long to dig, as does gravel. Loose soil takes only half as
 long.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Sphere of Annihilation:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A }{\i\fs24 sphere of annihilation}{\fs24 
 is a globe of absolute blackness, a ball of nothingness 2 feet in diameter. A sphere is actually a hole in the continuity of the multiverse, a void. Any matter that comes in contact with a sphere is instantly sucked into the void, gone, utterly destroyed
\emdash even }{\i\fs24 wishes}{\fs24  and similar magicks have no effect!
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 sphere of annihilation}{\fs24  is basically static, resting in some spot as if it were a normal hole. It can be caused to move, however, by mental effort. Th
e brain waves of the individual concentrating on moving it bend spatial fabrics, causing the hole to slide. Control range is 40 feet initially, 10 feet/level once control is established. Basic movement rate is 10 feet per round, modified as shown below.

\par \tab Concentration control is based on Intelligence and level of experience\emdash 
the higher the level the greater the mental power and discipline. For every point of Intelligence above 12, the wizard adds 1%; for every point over 15, he adds another 3%. In other word
s, add 1% for each point from 13 to 15, and an additional 3% for each point from 16-18\emdash a maximum of 12% bonus at 18 Intelligence. The bonus applies to this table:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx2160\tx4320\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 \tab \tab Probability of\tab 
