\par \tab 
Any attempt to control the sphere will cause it to move, but if control is not established, the sphere will slide toward the wizard attempting to move it. The sphere will continue to move in this direction for 1d4 rounds and as long as the wizard is withi
n 30 feet thereafter.
\par \tab If two or more wizards vie for control of a }{\i\fs24 sphere of annihilation,}{\fs24 
 the one with the highest percentage chance to control the sphere is checked first, then the next strongest, etc. Control chance is reduced 5% per person, cumulative, 
when two or more wizards concentrate on the sphere, even if they are cooperating. If none are successful, the sphere will slip toward the strongest. Control must be checked each round.
\par \tab Should a }{\i\fs24 gate}{\fs24  spell be cast upon a sphere, there is a 50% chance that 
the spell will destroy it, 35% that the spell will do nothing, and 15% that a gap will be torn in the spatial fabric, and everything in a 180-foot radius will be catapulted into another plane or universe.
\par \tab If a }{\i\fs24 rod of cancellation}{\fs24  touches a sphere, a tremendous explosion will occur as they negate each other. Everything within a 60-foot radius will sustain 3d4 x 10 points of damage.
\par \tab See also }{\i\fs24 talisman of the sphere,}{\fs24  below.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Stone Horse:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Each item of this nature appears to be full-sized, roughly hewn statue 
of a horse, carved from some type of hard stone. A command word brings the steed to life, enabling it to carry a burden, and even to attack as if it were a warhorse. There are two sorts of steeds:
\par 
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Courser:}{\fs24  This }{\i\fs24 stone horse}{\fs24  travels at the same movement ra
te as a light horse (movement rate 24) and attacks as if it were a medium warhorse (three attacks for 1d6/1d6/1d3). It is Armor Class 3 and has 18 hit points. It saves versus all applicable attack forms as if it were "Metal, hard."
\par \tab }{\i\fs24 Destrier:}{\fs24  This }{\i\fs24 stone horse}{\fs24 
 travels at the same movement rate as a medium horse (movement rate 18) and attacks as if it were a heavy warhorse (three attacks for 1d8/1d8/1d3). It is Armor Class 1 and has 26 hit points. It saves versus all applicable attack forms as if it were "Met
al, hard."
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 stone horse}{\fs24  can carry 1,000 pounds tirelessly and never needs to rest or feed. Damage inflicted upon it can be repaired by first using a }{\i\fs24 stone to flesh}{\fs24  spell, thus causing the }{\i\fs24 stone horse}{\fs24 
 to become a normal horse. If then allowed to graze and rest, the animal will heal its wounds at the rate of one point per day. When it is fully healed, it will automatically revert to its magical form.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Stone of Controlling Earth Elementals:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A stone of this nature is typically an oddly shaped bit of roughly 
polished rock. The possessor of such a stone need but utter a single command word, and an earth elemental of 12-Hit-Dice size will come to the summoner if earth is available, an 8-Hit-Dice elemental if rough, unhewn stone is the summoning medium. (An eart
h
 elemental cannot be summoned from worked stone, but one can be from mud, clay, or even sand, although one from sand is an eight-dice monster.) The area of summoning for an earth elemental must be at least 4 feet square and have four cubic yards volume. T
he elemental will appear in 1d4 rounds. For detailed information about elementals and their control see the }{\i\fs24 Monstrous Compendium.}{\fs24  The stone can be used to summon one elemental per day.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Stone of Good Luck (Luckstone):}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This magical stone is typically a bit 
of rough polished agate or similar mineral. Its possessor gains a +1 (+5% where applicable) on all dice rolls involving factors such as saving, slipping, dodging, etc.\emdash 
whenever dice are rolled to find whether the character suffers from some adverse happening. This luck does not affect attack and damage rolls or spell failure dice.
\par \tab Additionally, the }{\i\fs24 luckstone}{\fs24  gives the possessor a +/- 1% to 10% (at owner's option) on rolls for determination of magical items or diversion of treasure. The most favorable results will always be gained with a }{\i\fs24 
stone of good luck.}{\fs24 
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Stone of Weight (Loadstone):}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This magical stone appears to be any one of the other sorts, and testing will not reveal its nature. However, as soon as the possessor of a }{\i\fs24 stone of weight}{\fs24 
 is in a situation where he is required to move quickly in order to avoid an enemy\emdash combat or pursuit\emdash 
the item causes a 50% reduction in movement, and even attacks are reduced to 50% normal rate. Furthermore, the stone cannot be gotten rid of by any nonmagical means\emdash if it is
 thrown away or smashed, it will reappear somewhere on the character's person. If a }{\i\fs24 dispel evil}{\fs24  is cast upon a }{\i\fs24 loadstone,}{\fs24  the item will disappear and no longer haunt the individual.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Talisman of Pure Good:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A high priest who possesses this item can cause a
 flaming crack to open at the feet of an evil priest. The intended victim will be swallowed up forever and sent hurtling to the center of the earth. The wielder of the talisman must be good, and if he is not exceptionally pure in thought and deed, the evi
l priest will gain a saving throw vs. death.
\par \tab A }{\i\fs24 talisman of pure good}{\fs24  has seven charges. It cannot be recharged. If a neutral priest touches one of these magic stones, he will suffer 7d4 points of damage. If an evil priest touches one, he will suffer 12d4 p
oints of damage. Nonpriests will not be affected by the device.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Talisman of the Sphere:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This is a small adamantite loop and handle which will be useless to nonwizards. Characters of any other class touching a talisman of this sort will suffer 5d6 points 
of damage. When held by a wizard concentrating on control of a }{\i\fs24 sphere of annihilation,}{\fs24  a }{\i\fs24 talisman of the sphere}{\fs24 
 doubles the Intelligence bonus percentage for control (i.e., 2% per point of Intelligence from 13-15, 6% per point of Intelligence from 16-18).
\par \tab If control is established by the wielder of a talisman, he need check for continual control only every other round thereafter. If control is not established, the sphere will move toward the wizard at maximum speed (16 feet/round). Note that a }{
\i\fs24 wand of negation}{\fs24  will have no effect upon a }{\i\fs24 sphere of annihilation,}{\fs24  but if the wand is directed at the talisman it will negate its power of control as long as the wand is directed at it.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Talisman of Ultimate Evil:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This device exactly resembles a }{\i\fs24 talisman of pure good}{\fs24  and is exactly its opposite in all respects. It has six charges.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Talisman of Zagy:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A talisman of this sort appears exactly the same as a }{\i\fs24 stone of controlling earth elementals.}{\fs24  Its powers are quite different, however, and are depende
nt upon the Charisma of the individual holding the talisman. Whenever a character touches a }{\i\fs24 talisman of Zagy,}{\fs24  a reaction check is made as if the individual were meeting another creature.
\par \tab If a hostile reaction result is obtained, the device will act as a }{\i\fs24 stone of weight,}{\fs24  although discarding it or destroying it results only in 5d6 points of damage and the disappearance of the talisman.
\par \tab If a neutral reaction results, the talisman will remain with the character for 5d6 hours, or until a }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  is made upon it, whichever first occurs, and it will then disappear.
\par \tab If a friendly reaction result is obtained, the character will find it impossible to be rid of the talisman for as many months as he has points of Charisma.
\par \tab The device will grant one }{\i\fs24 wish}{\fs24  for every six p
oints of the character's Charisma. It will also grow warm and throb whenever its possessor comes within 20 feet of a mechanical or magical trap. (If the talisman is not held, its warning heat and pulses will be of no avail.)
\par \tab Regardless of which reaction result is obtained, when its time period expires, the talisman will disappear. A base 10,000 gp diamond will remain in its stead.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Tome of Clear Thought:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A work of this nature is indistinguishable from any normal book. Any single character who reads a }{\i\fs24 tome of clear thought}{\fs24 
 will be able to practice mental exercises that will increase his Intelligence by one point. Reading a work of this nature takes 48 hours time over six days, and immediately thereafter the book disappears.
\par \tab The reader must begin a program 
of concentration and mental discipline within one week of reading the tome. After a month of such exercise, Intelligence goes up. The knowledge gained from reading the work can never be recorded or articulated. Any further perusal of a }{\i\fs24 
tome of clear thought}{\fs24  will be of no benefit to the character.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Tome of Leadership and Influence:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This leather-and-brass-bound book is similar to a }{\i\fs24 tome of clear thought}{\fs24 
, but upon completion of reading and practice of what was revealed therein, Charisma is increased by one point.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Tome of Understanding:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Identical to a }{\i\fs24 tome of clear thought,}{\fs24  this work increases Wisdom by one point.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Universal Solvent:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This strange and magical liquid appears to be some sort of minor oil or potion. Upon first examination, it seems to have the properties of both }{\i\fs24 oil of slipperiness}{\fs24 
 and a potion of }{\i\fs24 delusion.}{\fs24  However, if it is applied to any form of adhesive or sticky material, the solution will immediately dissolve it. Thus, for instance, the effect of }{\i\fs24 sovereign glue}{\fs24 
 will immediately be negated by this liquid, as will any other form of cement, glue, or adhesive. The area of effect of this liquid is one cubic foot per ounce, and a typical container holds 27 ounces.
\par \tab If the liquid is carefully distilled to bring it down to one-third of its original volume, each ounce will dissolve one cubic foot of organic or inorganic material, just as if a }{\i\fs24 disintegrate}{\fs24 
 spell had been employed. To find if a target is affected by this concentrated solution, a normal attack roll is requir
ed, and the subject is entitled to a saving throw vs. spell. Inanimate objects are automatically affected by the solution, although if they are magical, a saving throw vs. disintegrate applies.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Vacuous Grimoire:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A book of this sort is identical to a normal one, although if a }{\i\fs24 detect magic}{\fs24 
 spell is cast, a magical aura will be noted. Any character who opens the work and reads so much as a single glyph therein must make two saving throws vs. spell. The first is to determine if one point of Intelligence is lo
st or not; the second is to find if two points of Wisdom are lost. Once opened and read, the }{\i\fs24 vacuous grimoire}{\fs24  remains; to be destroyed, the book must be burned and a }{\i\fs24 remove curse}{\fs24 
 spell cast. If the tome is placed with other books, its appearance will instantly alter to conform to the look of these other works.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Well of Many Worlds:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This strange interdimensional device looks just like a }{\i\fs24 portable hole.}{\fs24  Anything placed within it is immediately cast to another world\emdash 
a parallel earth, another planet, or a different plane at the DM's option or by random determination. If the well is moved, the random factor again comes into play. It can be picked up, folded, etc., just like a }{\i\fs24 portable hole.}{\fs24 
 Things from the world the well touches can come through the opening, just as easily as from the initiating place.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wind Fan:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 A }{\i\fs24 wind fan}{\fs24 
 appears to be nothing more than a wood and papyrus or cloth instrument with which to create a cooling breeze. The possessor can, however, by uttering the correct word, cause the fan to generate air movement duplicating a }{\i\fs24 gust of wind}{\fs24 
 spell as if cast by a 5th-level wizard. The fan can be used once per day with no risk. If it is used more frequently, there is a cumulative 20% chance per usage that the device will tear into useless, nonmagical tatters.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Wings of Flying:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
A pair of these magical wings appear to be nothing more than a plain cloak of old, black cloth. If the wearer speaks a command word, the cloak will turn into a pair of gigantic bat wings (20-foot span) and empower the wearer to fly as follows:
\par 
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx270\adjustright {\fs24 \tab   2 turns at speed 32
\par \tab   3 turns at speed 25
\par \tab   4 turns at speed 18
\par \tab   6 turns at speed 15
\par \tab   8 turns at speed 12
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab After the maximum number of possible turns flying, the wearer must rest for one hour\emdash 
sitting, lying down, or sleeping. Shorter periods of flight do not require full rest, but only relative quiet such as slow walking for one hour. Any flight of less than one turn's duration does not require any rest. }{\i\fs24 Wings of flying}{\fs24 
 can be used just once per day regardless of the length of time spent flying. They will support up to 500 pounds weight.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf12 Armor and Shields
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab In this section, you will find descriptions of some very special kinds of armor and shields. Before adding these to your campaign, however, be sure you are fully familiar with the armor gradation system:
\par \tab For each +1 bonus to armor, regardless of the type of armor, the wearer's Armor Class moves downward (toward AC 2 . . . to 1 . . . to 0, -1, -2, and so on). A normal shield improves the arm
or class by one. A magical shield improves Armor Class like magical armor\emdash toward -1, -2, etc. Note, however, that Armor Class can never be improved beyond -10.
\par \tab Thus, }{\i\fs24 chain mail +1}{\fs24  is like ordinary chain mail (AC 5), but one category better (AC 4). A }{\i\fs24 shield +1}{\fs24  is equal to Armor Class 8\emdash 
two places better than no armor (+1 for bearing a shield, +1 for the magical bonus of the shield).
\par \tab When adding magical armor to the game, be aware of sizing problems: 65% of all armor (except }{\i\fs24 elven chain mail}{\fs24 ) is man-sized, and 20% is elf-sized, 10% is dwarf-sized, and but 5% gnome- or halfling-sized. (}{\i\fs24 Elven chain mail}
{\fs24  sizing is determined by the table found below.)
\par \tab Special armor and shields are described below:
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Armor of Blending:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This appears to be a normal suit of magica
l armor (determine type and AC modifier normally, ignoring negative results). However, upon command (a command word can be assigned if the DM desires), the armor changes shape and form, assuming the appearance of a normal set of clothing. The armor retain
s all its properties (including weight) when disguised. Only a }{\i\fs24 true seeing}{\fs24  spell will reveal the true nature of the armor when disguised.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Armor of Command:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This finely crafted plate mail radiates a powerful aura of magic. When worn, the armor bestows a di
gnified and commanding aura upon its owner. The wearer is treated as if he had a Charisma of 18 for all encounter reactions. Friendly troops within 360 feet of the user have their morale increased by +2. Otherwise, the armor functions as }{\i\fs24 
plate mail +1.}{\fs24  Since the effect arises in great part from the distinctiveness of the armor, the wearer cannot hide or conceal himself in any way and still have the effect function.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Armor of Missile Attraction:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This armor appears to be a normal suit of magical armor (deter
mine type and modifier normally). However, the armor is cursed and actually serves to attract missiles. The wearer is two or three times more likely to be selected as a random target of missiles than normal. In cases where each person is the target of a s
e
t number of missiles (most often in large combats), the wearer will have a greater number of missiles fired at him. Furthermore, the magical protection of the armor will fail when calculated for missile attacks. The true nature of the armor will not revea
l itself until the character is fired upon in earnest\emdash simple experiments (throwing rocks, etc.) will not suffice.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Armor of Rage:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This armor is identical in appearance to }{\i\fs24 armor of command}{\fs24  and functions as a suit of }{\i\fs24 plate mail +1}{\fs24 . However, when worn, the ar
mor causes the character to suffer a -3 penalty to all encounter checks. All friendly troops within 360 feet have their morale lowered by -2. The effect is not noticeable to the wearer or those affected (i.e., characters will not immediately notice that d
onning the armor is the cause of their problems).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Elven Chain Mail:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This is magical armor so fine and light that it can be worn under normal clothing without revealing its presence. Its lightness and flexibility allow even bards and thieves to use it with
 few restrictions (see Chapter 3 in the }{\i\fs24 PHB}{\fs24 
). Elven fighter/mages use it without restriction. However, it is rarely sized to fit anyone other than an elf or a half-elf. Roll percentile dice and consult the following table to ascertain what size character }{\i\fs24 elven chain mail}{\fs24 
 will fit:
\par 
\par }\pard \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright {\b\fs24\cf6 D100 Roll\tab Size of Elven Chain Mail}{\fs24\cf6 \tab 
\par }\pard\plain \s15\keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright {01-10\tab gnome/halfling (hairfoot)
\par }\pard\plain \keep\nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx1440\adjustright \fs20 {\fs24 11-15\tab dwarf/halfling (Stout or Tallfellow)
\par 16-80\tab elf/half-elf
\par 81-95\tab man-sized, normal (up to 6 feet, 200 lbs.)
\par 96-00\tab man-sized, large (up to 6\'bd feet, 250 lbs.)
\par }\pard \nowidctlpar\tx-720\tx240\tx480\adjustright {\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Plate Mail of Etherealness:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This is seemingly normal }{\i\fs24 plate mail +5,}{\fs24 
 but if a command word is spoken, the suit enables its wearer and all nonliving items he wears and carries to become ethereal, as if }{\i\fs24 oil of etherealness}{\fs24  had been used. While in the etherea
l state the wearer cannot attack material creatures. A }{\i\fs24 phase door}{\fs24  spell will negate the ethereal state and prevent the armor from functioning for one day.
\par \tab There are 20 charges placed upon }{\i\fs24 plate mail of etherealness.}{\fs24  Once used, these cannot be replaced or 
recharged. Furthermore, every five uses reduces the bonus of the armor by one: If five charges are used to become ethereal, the armor is +4, if 10 are used it is +3, +2 if 15 are used, and only +1 if all 20 are exhausted.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Plate Mail of Fear:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This armor functions as normal }{\i\fs24 plate mail +1.}{\fs24  However, it is imbued with 2d23 }{\i\fs24 fear}{\fs24 
 charges. Upon uttering the command, a charge is spent and the wearer radiates a 30-foot aura of fear. All creatures (except the one wearing the armor) must save vs. spell or flee in pan
ic for 1d4+1 rounds. When all charges are spent, the armor functions as normal }{\i\fs24 plate mail +1.}{\fs24  It cannot be recharged.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Plate Mail of Vulnerability:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This appears to every test to be magical }{\i\fs24 plate mail +1, +2, or +3,}{\fs24  but it is actually }{\i\fs24 cursed plate mail -2, -3, or -4.}{\fs24 
 The armor's great vulnerability will not be apparent until an enemy successfully strikes a blow in anger with desire and intent to kill the wearer. The armor will fall to pieces whenever an opponent strikes the wearer with 
an unmodified attack roll of 20.
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Shield, large, +1, +4 versus missiles:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 
This is a large shield with a +1 bonus vs. melee attacks, but it is four times more effective against hand-hurled and mechanically propelled missiles of all sorts. More importantly, the shield has a 20% chance of negating }{\i\fs24 magic missile}{\fs24 
 attacks (from a frontal position).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Shield -1, missile attractor:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This not only makes the bearer equivalent to a shieldless person, it also attracts missiles of all types to itself: It doubles or tripl
es the bearer's chances of being selected by random die rolling according to the size of the party he is with (see Chapter 9, "Firing into a Melee"). This cursed shield is not distinguishable from a useful magical shield.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf12 Magical Weapons
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab Magical weapons normally apply their bonuses (+1, +2, +3, or +4) to both attack and damage rolls. Any weapon that is not totally self-explanatory is detailed in one of the special paragraphs below.
\par \tab Most weapons are of the specific type listed. However, 70% of swords ar
e long swords, 20% are scimitars or broad swords, 5% are short (small) swords, 4% are bastard swords, and 1% are two-handed swords, unless the description of a specific item indicates otherwise. Magical polearms can be of any type desired. Magical lances 
are always heavy lances. Other unusual weapons may have special restrictions given in the description of the item.
\par \tab Spears can be used as hand or missile weapons. As missile weapons, they can be broken by any creature with 18/00 or greater Strength or by on
e that is massive (cave bear, hippo, or rhino, for example). Intelligent creatures will be 70% likely to use the hurled spear against the hurler if struck by the weapon, 25% likely to break it (the spear must save versus }{\i\fs24 crushing blow}{\fs24 
). Unintelligent creatures will be 25% likely to break it (save as above).
\par \tab Hand Axes (not battle-axes) can be thrown up to 30 yards and still gain any magical attack roll bonus, but no damage bonus.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Light Generation
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab The DM can rule that magical weapons (particularly swords, daggers, and +3 or greater items) shed light and can't be concealed when drawn. However, a magical sword should never glow until the wielder is aware of its magical properties\emdash 
the light-giving property of a weapon should never be used as an excuse to identify a magical item. Note that the }{\i\fs24 flame tongue, frost brand, holy avenger, life stealing,}{\fs24  and }{\i\fs24 sharpness}{\fs24 
 swords have special properties with regard to light. These are dealt with in the individual weapon descriptions.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 Unknown or Unusual Qualities
\par 
\par }{\fs24 \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Intelligent Weapons:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 One of the rare and more significant properties of a magical weapon is the chance that it is intelligent. The feature is most common among swords, but there are occasional instances o
f other weapons possessing intelligence. The chance of intelligence varies according to the type of weapon.
\par \tab Swords have a 25% chance of some form of intelligence. Other melee weapons (axes, spears, polearms, etc.) have a 5% chance of intelligence, provide
d they do not already possess special powers. Missile weapons (including bows, crossbows, arrows, and bolts) never possess intelligence. Single-use items and those items that do not have a bonus to hit (such as a magical net) never possess intelligence.

\par \tab If you determine that a weapon is intelligent, it will have one or more special powers. It may also have a special purpose or limitations on its use. This information is found under "Intelligent Weapons."
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Unknown Qualities:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 Whenever a weapon has some unknown quality\emdash such as the }{\i\fs24 wishes}{\fs24  in a }{\i\fs24 luck blade}{\fs24 \emdash 
the DM should prepare a special 3-inch by 5-inch index card on it and keep the information handy whenever the possessor of the weapon is playing.
\par }{\b\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Magical arrows:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 When a magical arrow misses its target, there is a 50% chance it will break or otherwise be rendered useless. A magical arrow that hits is destroyed.
\par 
\par }{\fs36\cf6 List of Magical Weapons
\par }{\fs24 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Arrow of Direction:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This typically appears to be a normal arrow. However, its magical properties make it function like a }{\i\fs24 locate object}{\fs24 
 spell, empowering the arrow to show the direction to the nearest stairway, passage, cave, etc.
\par \tab Once per day the device can be tossed into the air; it will fall and point in the requested direction. This process can be repeated seven times during the next seven turns. The request must be for one of the following:
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 Stairway (up or down)
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 Sloping passage (up or down)
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 Dungeon exit or entrance
\par \tab   }{\b\fs24 \bullet  }{\fs24 Cave or cavern
\par \tab Requests must be phrased by distance (nearest, farthest, highest, lowest) or by direction (north, south, east, west, etc.).
\par 
\par \tab }{\b\fs24\cf6 Arrow of Slaying:}{\fs24\cf6  }{\fs24 This is an }{\i\fs24 arrow +3}{\fs24  with unusual physical characteristics\emdash 
a shaft of some special material, feathers of some rare creature, a head of some strange design, a rune carved on the nock, etc.
 These characteristics indicate the arrow is effective against some creature type. If the arrow is employed against the kind of creature it has been enchanted to slay, the missile will kill it instantly if it hits the target creature. The following list c
omprises only a portion of the possible kinds of these arrows:
\par 
